// ManAlert — alert (generated from docs/shell/man-alert.md).
const ManAlert = "ALERT(1)                        Weft Commands                         ALERT(1)\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mNAME\x1b[0m\n  alert — evaluate conditions and dispatch notifications\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mSYNOPSIS\x1b[0m\n    <pipeline> | alert [<condition>] [--notify=<alias>]\n                       [-k <field>] [-t|--tag <field>]\n                       [--duration <dur>] [--average] [--msg <text>]\n\n    <pipeline> | alert (--if-zero <v> | --if-nonzero <v>) [--notify=<alias>]\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mDESCRIPTION\x1b[0m\n  Reads a value from stdin and evaluates it against a condition.\n  If the condition is met, writes an alert message to stderr and,\n  when --notify is supplied, dispatches the alias from /etc/notify.yaml\n  to its Slack, PagerDuty, webhook, and/or email targets.\n\n  If no condition is given, the default is \"any\": the alert fires whenever\n  stdin is non-empty. alert alone is a dry run that writes to stderr.\n\n  When the condition is not met, stdin is passed through to stdout\n  unchanged. When it is met, the alert message goes to stderr.\n\n  alert must be the last stage of its pipeline, and the last (or only)\n  command of a script.\n\n  If the pipeline ahead of alert fails (an unknown source, a bad stage),\n  the alert is not evaluated. The shell shows the error, and a cron run is\n  recorded with status error and opens no incident. A pipeline that runs\n  fine and prints nothing (a grep with no match) gives alert empty input,\n  and absent fires on it.\n\n  In a cron (/cron.d/), incidents are deduplicated on\n  (cron, condition). Later ticks during an open incident\n  update it and send no new page. When the condition clears, the\n  incident closes.\n\n  From the interactive shell, alert is the supported way to\n  test an alias end-to-end:\n\n    echo \"hello\" | alert --notify=ops\n\n  Without --notify, alert writes to stderr (dry-run mode).\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mMULTI-ROW MODE\x1b[0m\n  By default alert treats stdin as a single scalar value per tick.\n  With -k, each non-blank line of stdin is evaluated independently,\n  and -k <field> picks the value field. With -t <field> (a.k.a.\n  --tag), another field names the row; each distinct tag opens\n  and closes incidents, and dispatches notifications, independently.\n  Use -t whenever the pipeline can produce more than one row per\n  tick; rows without a tag share one incident.\n\n  <field> is one of:\n\n  - A 1-indexed whitespace column (-k 2 → second whitespace-split\n    field). Lines are split on whitespace; pre-format with awk/sed/jq\n    so each row is one line of space-separated values.\n  - A .-prefixed JSON path (-k .latency_ms, -k .req.host). Each\n    line is parsed as a single JSON value. Useful when the upstream\n    is jq emitting JSONL.\n\n  A single alert may not mix the two forms across -k and -t.\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mCONDITIONS\x1b[0m\n  - any — fires whenever stdin is non-empty (the default when --notify is given)\n  - above <N> — current value is greater than N\n  - below <N> — current value is less than N\n  - eq <N> — current value equals N\n  - neq <N> — current value does not equal N\n  - changed — value differs from previous run\n  - absent — no input received in the window\n  - rising <N>% — value increased by N% compared to previous/window average\n  - falling <N>% — value decreased by N% compared to previous/window average\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mCOMPOSITE PREDICATES\x1b[0m\n  A cron that needs to fire on the state of more than one cron composes\n  predicate builtins (weft::is_above, weft::is_absent, etc. — see\n  man weft-funcs) with bash && || ! inside a subshell. Ending the\n  subshell with echo makes it print something only when the whole\n  condition holds, and alert's default any condition fires on that:\n\n    # /cron.d/deploy-danger\n    # schedule: every 1m\n    (weft::is_above error-rate 50 && weft::is_absent canary-ping && echo fire) | alert --notify=critical\n\n  To combine conditions on the logs themselves, count each in one pass\n  over the cron's time range and alert on the result (see \"Alerting\n  strategies\").\n\n  --if-zero <value> fires when the value is \"0\" (the convention bash uses\n  for success, matching the right-hand interpretation of && chains).\n  --if-nonzero <value> is the inverse. The two are mutually exclusive\n  with the stdin condition keywords above.\n\n  See the Alerting strategies manual page (man alerting-strategies) for the full pattern\n  catalogue (single-cron scalar, multi-row per-tag, composite,\n  aggregated-value).\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mOPTIONS\x1b[0m\n  - --notify=<alias> — dispatch to the named alias in /etc/notify.yaml. Without --notify, alert writes to stderr.\n  - --if-zero <value> / --if-nonzero <value> — fire based on a literal exit-code value (typically \"$?\" from the prior compound). Mutually exclusive with stdin condition keywords.\n  - -k <field> — multi-row mode: each line of stdin is one row, and <field> selects the numeric value. <field> is either a 1-indexed whitespace column (e.g. 2) or a .-prefixed JSON path against the line parsed as JSON (e.g. .req.latency_ms).\n  - -t <field>, --tag <field> — multi-row mode: <field> names this row for per-tag incident dedup and per-tag notifications. Same field grammar as -k. Must agree with -k on positional vs JSON-path. Required when the pipeline can produce more than one row per tick.\n  - --duration <dur> — time window for sustained conditions. For above/below: condition must hold for ALL runs in the window. For rising/falling: compares against the window average. For absent: no non-empty output in the window. Units are s, m and h only: 5m, 1h, 30s (1d is rejected).\n  - --average — for above/below with --duration: compare the rolling average over the window against the threshold. It has no effect without --duration.\n  - --msg <text> — override the default alert message.\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mEXAMPLES\x1b[0m\n    # Fire if the range holds any ERROR line.\n    cat /log/myco/prod/api/main.log | grep ERROR | alert --notify=ops\n\n    # Fire when the range holds more than 50 errors.\n    cat /log/myco/prod/api/main.log | grep ERROR | wc -l | alert above 50 --notify=critical\n\n    # check_errors stands for a script of yours in /bin that prints one number.\n    check_errors | alert above 50 --duration 5m --average --notify=ops\n    check_errors | alert rising 100% --notify=critical\n\n    # Fire when the range holds no canary line at all.\n    cat /log/myco/prod/canary/main.log | alert absent --notify=ops\n\n    echo \"integration test\" | alert --notify=ops\n\n    # Multi-row positional: alert per host whose disk use is above 80%.\n    cat /log/myco/prod/api/disk.log \\\n        | jq -r '\"\\($host) \\(.pct)\"' \\\n        | alert above 80 -k 2 -t 1 --notify=ops\n\n    # Multi-row JSON path: same idea with JSON rows. The host is a column,\n    # not a field of the row, so build the row with it.\n    cat /log/myco/prod/api/disk.log | jq -c '{host: $host, pct}' \\\n        | alert above 80 -k .pct -t .host --notify=ops\n\n  In a cron, count over the cron's own time range. A row limit with\n  head -n or tail -n can silently truncate the window.\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mSEE ALSO\x1b[0m\n  help, help alerts, help cron, man weft-funcs, man range, vi, cat\n"

// ManChart — chart (generated from docs/shell/man-chart.md).
const ManChart = "CHART(1)                        Weft Commands                         CHART(1)\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mNAME\x1b[0m\n  chart — render a chart in the terminal, from a cron or a pipeline\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mSYNOPSIS\x1b[0m\n    chart <cron> [-t TYPE] [-n LIMIT] [-i INTERVAL] [-f|--follow]\n    <pipeline> | chart [-x <field>] [-y <field>] [-a <fn>] [--tag <field>]\n                       [--bucket <dur>] [-t TYPE] [--title <text>]\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mDESCRIPTION\x1b[0m\n  chart runs in two forms:\n\n  Cron form — chart <cron> reads recent results from a\n  /cron.d/<cron> and paints a chart in the terminal. The cron\n  name can be given bare (chart errors) or as a path\n  (chart /cron.d/errors). One-shot renders a single frame and\n  returns; --follow enters a live-redraw mode that refreshes every\n  <INTERVAL> until you press q (or Esc, Ctrl+C,\n  Ctrl+D).\n\n  Pipe form — <pipeline> | chart [...] renders the upstream\n  output once and returns to the prompt. The chart stage is the\n  last stage of the pipeline; piping its output onward is an error.\n  Use it to sketch a chart before saving the pipeline as a cron.\n  --follow is not supported in pipe form.\n\n  Charts fit the terminal and are never paged through less. A resize\n  re-fits in cron --follow mode on the next tick. There is no zoom or\n  scroll; the chart shows the configured window of data. When a bar\n  chart or sparkline has more points than the terminal has columns,\n  neighbouring points are merged into one bar each, re-applying -a to\n  their rows (count/sum add up, min/max keep dips and spikes; with\n  no -a, values are averaged), and a footer line says how many points\n  went into each bar. Line charts always fit and are never merged.\n\n  In cron form, the data point for each result is the last\n  whitespace-separated field of the cron's stdout, the same\n  convention as multi-row alert (alert -k <N>). The cron's output must\n  end in a number on each line; a cron that emits JSON rows cannot be\n  charted. In pipe form, -x and\n  -y pick fields explicitly; when omitted, x is the row sequence\n  and y is the last whitespace field of each line.\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mOPTIONS (cron form)\x1b[0m\n  - -t, --type <line|sparkline|bar> — chart kind. Default line.\n    sparkline is a single-line ramp; bar plots one bar per result\n    with the timestamp under it.\n  - -n, --limit <N> — number of recent results to fetch. Default 50.\n  - -i, --interval <DURATION> — redraw interval in --follow\n    mode. Default 1s. Minimum 100ms.\n  - -f, --follow — drop into live-redraw mode. The chart re-fetches\n    and repaints every <INTERVAL> until you press q.\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mOPTIONS (pipe form)\x1b[0m\n  - -x <field>[::time[(<format>)]] — x source. <field> is either a\n    1-indexed whitespace column (-x 1) or a .-prefixed JSON path\n    (-x .ts). Default: row sequence (0, 1, 2, …). An RFC3339 timestamp\n    is recognised as time on its own; anything else is a plain number or\n    label unless cast:\n      - ::time — x is a time: RFC3339 or epoch seconds (-x 1::time).\n      - ::time(<format>) — x is a time in a strftime format: %Y year,\n        %m month, %d day, %H hour, %M minute, %S second, %b\n        month name, %z UTC offset, %s epoch seconds, and the rest of\n        the usual date +FORMAT codes. A stamp with no zone is read as\n        UTC. A row that doesn't match the format stops the chart with an\n        error.\n      - With a time cast, a column range <start>-<end> joins those\n        columns with a space before parsing, for stamps that span two\n        fields: -x '1-2::time(%Y/%m/%d %H:%M:%S)'.\n\n  - -y <field> — y source. Same grammar as -x. Default: last\n    whitespace field of each line. -x and -y may not mix\n    positional with JSON-path in the same call. Casts are only\n    supported on -x.\n  - -a, --aggregate <fn> — y reduction within an x-group.\n    One of count, sum, avg, min, max, p50, p95,\n    identity. Default identity, or count when --bucket is set.\n  - --tag <field> — split output into one line per distinct value of\n    <field> — e.g. one line per host, one per\n    coordinator instance. Same field grammar as -x/-y; all three\n    must agree on positional vs JSON-path. Only valid with -t line\n    (the default when --tag is set). Each line gets its own color and\n    a legend row.\n  - --bucket <DURATION> — group rows into time buckets on x.\n    Implies -x …::time: x must parse as RFC3339, epoch seconds, or the\n    cast's format. With -a count (the default) or sum, a bucket with\n    no rows is drawn as 0, so a stretch with no logs shows as a hole.\n    Switches the default chart kind to line.\n  - -t, --type <line|sparkline|bar> — chart kind. Default bar,\n    or line when --bucket or --tag is set.\n  - --title <text> — chart title.\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mEXAMPLES\x1b[0m\n    # Cron form\n    chart error-rate                        one-shot line chart\n    chart error-rate -t sparkline           one-line glyph\n    chart error-rate -t bar                 vertical bars by timestamp\n    chart error-rate --follow               live; press q to exit\n\n    # Pipe form: a quick distribution by host\n    cat /log/myco/prod/api/host.log | jq -c '{host: $host, pct: .memory.pct}' \\\n      | chart -x .host -y .pct -a avg\n\n    # Pipe form: time-bucketed p95 over the current range\n    cat /log/myco/prod/api/host.log \\\n      | jq -c '{ts: $ts, pct: .memory.pct}' \\\n      | chart -x .ts -y .pct --bucket 1m -a p95\n\n    # Pipe form: log lines per minute, to spot a window with no logs.\n    # The stamp \"2026/10/13 15:04:05\" spans columns 1 and 2.\n    cat /log/myco/prod/api/main.log \\\n      | chart -x '1-2::time(%Y/%m/%d %H:%M:%S)' --bucket 1m\n\n    # Pipe form: plot a stream of numbers from jq\n    cat /log/myco/prod/api/host.log | jq '.memory.pct' | chart\n\n    # Pipe form: one line per instance\n    cat /log/myco/prod/api/host.log \\\n      | jq -c '{ts: $ts, host: $host, pct: .memory.pct}' \\\n      | chart -x .ts -y .pct --tag .host --bucket 1m -a avg\n\n    # --tag requires one JSON object per line, so use jq -c\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mRELATED\x1b[0m\n  ls -l /cron.d/ adds a colored sparkline beside each cron name\n  using the same rendering primitives.\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mSEE ALSO\x1b[0m\n  help, help cron, vi, cat\n"

// ManClipboard — clipboard (generated from docs/shell/man-clipboard.md).
const ManClipboard = "CLIPBOARD(1)                    Weft Commands                     CLIPBOARD(1)\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mNAME\x1b[0m\n  clipboard — copy a short, shareable link to a /bin script\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mSYNOPSIS\x1b[0m\n    clipboard /bin/<name>\n    clipboard /bin/<name> --range=<spec>\n    clipboard /bin/<name> -- <script-args>...\n    clipboard /bin/<name> --range=<spec> -- <script-args>...\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mDESCRIPTION\x1b[0m\n  clipboard records the current shell's view of a /bin/ script and\n  pushes a short link to it (https://w3ft.net/s/k3Fz9aQ2xB; the host depends on your deployment) onto the\n  system clipboard. A teammate who opens the link — signed in to the\n  same org — lands in the web shell with the script invocation\n  pre-filled, ready to run with a single Enter. The script must already\n  exist under /bin. Anyone outside the org\n  gets \"that link doesn't exist\".\n\n  Weft stores, behind the short name:\n\n  - the script path (/bin/<name>),\n  - the time range used for log queries,\n  - any positional or flag args you typed after --.\n\n  Sharing the same script, range and args again gives the same link.\n\n  The recipient sees the script invocation in their prompt, can edit\n  it, and presses Enter to run it. Scripts resolve to the latest\n  version when the recipient runs them.\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mRANGE\x1b[0m\n  Without --range, clipboard captures the shell's current range\n  (the window range shows). To override, pass --range=<spec> using\n  the same syntax as the range command — 2h.., 1d..,\n  2026-04-12..2026-04-13, etc. See man range.\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mSCRIPT ARGS\x1b[0m\n  Everything after a -- separator becomes the script's arguments,\n  joined with single spaces. Other whitespace and shell quoting from\n  the original invocation are not preserved.\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mERRORS\x1b[0m\n  clipboard fails with no such script /bin/<name> if the script does\n  not exist, first argument must be a /bin/<name> path for any other\n  first argument, and invalid range for a bad --range.\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mCLIPBOARD MECHANISM\x1b[0m\n  The command emits an OSC-52 escape sequence containing the link.\n  Modern terminal emulators (iTerm2, kitty, WezTerm, xterm with\n  OSC52 enabled, the weft web shell) intercept it and write to the\n  system clipboard. Terminals without OSC-52 support ignore the\n  sequence. The link is also printed to the screen so you can select\n  and copy it manually.\n\n  Over SSH, OSC-52 works: the escape sequence travels with the\n  rest of the terminal output and the local terminal performs the\n  clipboard write.\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mEXAMPLES\x1b[0m\n    clipboard /bin/error-rate\n                            link with current range, no args\n\n    clipboard /bin/error-rate -- --app=loom --since=1h\n                            link that pre-fills error-rate --app=loom --since=1h\n\n    clipboard /bin/scan --range=24h..\n                            link that pins a 24-hour window regardless of\n                            the sharer's current range\n\n    clipboard /bin/check --range=yesterday..today -- prod\n                            link to /bin/check with one positional arg\n                            and yesterday's window\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mSEE ALSO\x1b[0m\n  range, git\n"

// ManDashboard — dashboard (generated from docs/shell/man-dashboard.md).
const ManDashboard = "DASHBOARD(1)                    Weft Commands                     DASHBOARD(1)\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mNAME\x1b[0m\n  dashboard — view a named tile layout in the terminal\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mSYNOPSIS\x1b[0m\n    dashboard\n    dashboard <name>\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mDESCRIPTION\x1b[0m\n  With no arguments, lists available dashboards.\n\n  With a name, enters an interactive full-screen view. Arrow keys\n  move focus between tiles; Enter zooms the focused tile to full\n  screen; Esc exits zoom or the view.\n\n  Dashboards are YAML files under /etc/dashboards/. Create one\n  with vi /etc/dashboards/<name>.yaml; writing under /etc needs the\n  configmgr group.\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mYAML GRAMMAR\x1b[0m\n    title: Production\n    sections:\n      - title: Errors\n        tiles:\n          - kind: sparkline\n            cron: error-rate\n            size: md\n          - kind: counter\n            cron: pending-jobs\n            threshold: { warn: 100, crit: 500 }\n            format: \"%d jobs\"\n            size: sm\n          - kind: table\n            cron: top-errors\n            columns: [host, count]\n            size: lg\n      - title: Capacity\n        tiles:\n          - kind: gauge\n            cron: disk-pct\n            threshold: { warn: 80, crit: 95, max: 100 }\n\n  Each cron: must name a cron that already exists (ls /cron.d); the\n  names above are examples.\n\n  A flat tiles: list (no sections:) is shorthand for one untitled\n  section. You cannot mix sections: and top-level tiles:.\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mTOP-LEVEL KEYS\x1b[0m\n  - title — optional heading at the top of the dashboard.\n  - sections — list of tile groups, each with an optional title and a tiles list.\n  - tiles — shorthand for a single untitled section.\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mSECTION KEYS\x1b[0m\n  - title — optional heading rendered above the section's tiles.\n  - tiles — required list of tile entries.\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mTILE KEYS\x1b[0m\n  - kind — one of sparkline, counter, gauge, table.\n  - cron — required; name of an existing cron (must exist when saved).\n    A tile that uses watch: is an error that names cron:.\n  - title — optional label for the tile.\n  - size — sm, md, or lg. Default md. A layout hint.\n  - format — (counter only) printf-style format, e.g. \"%d jobs\", \"%.1f%%\".\n  - threshold — (counter, gauge, sparkline) warn and crit cutoffs; gauge also takes max.\n  - columns — (table only, required) header labels; rows are whitespace-split from the cron stdout.\n  - tag, y — (sparkline only, set together) for a cron that prints JSON rows: tag\n    names the field that says which line a row belongs to, y the field to plot.\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mTHRESHOLD KEYS\x1b[0m\n  - warn — value at or above this renders yellow.\n  - crit — value at or above this renders red.\n  - max — (gauge only) upper bound of the bar. Default 100.\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mEXAMPLES\x1b[0m\n    dashboard ops               open the \"ops\" dashboard (create it first)\n    vi /etc/dashboards/ops.yaml create or edit it\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mSEE ALSO\x1b[0m\n  help, help cron, man chart\n"

// ManExplain — explain (generated from docs/shell/man-explain.md).
const ManExplain = "EXPLAIN(1)                      Weft Commands                       EXPLAIN(1)\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mNAME\x1b[0m\n  explain — show how much of a pipeline is optimized\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mSYNOPSIS\x1b[0m\n    explain <pipeline>\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mDESCRIPTION\x1b[0m\n  explain takes a one-liner, exactly as you would run it, and says which of its\n  stages are optimized and what would make more of them so. It does not run the\n  pipeline, reads no logs, and is not limited by range.\n\n  A pipeline is fastest when its stages are part of the optimized\n  retrieval: they narrow and reshape rows as the logs are fetched, so only the\n  result comes back to you. Any other stage runs post-retrieval, on every row\n  that came back. Each stage is reported as one of:\n\n    optimized retrieval     part of the retrieval; only its result comes back\n    partly optimized        its filter is optimized retrieval; the rest of the\n                            stage runs post-retrieval\n    post-retrieval          runs on rows that have already come back\n\n  The first stage that is not entirely optimized is the barrier. Every stage\n  after it runs post-retrieval, so the useful change is nearly always at or\n  before the barrier. A stage that is not optimized says why, and where there is\n  something to change, what to try.\n\n  When every stage is optimized retrieval, explain says so and there is nothing\n  to gain.\n\n  Relative paths resolve against the current directory, as they do for any\n  command. A script in /bin is explained through its body.\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mEXAMPLES\x1b[0m\n    $ explain cat /log/acme/api/main.log | awk '$3 == \"ingest-stream:\" { printf \"%s\\n\", $0 }' | grep 'first row'\n      1  awk '$3 == \"ingest-stream:\" { printf \"%s\\n\", $0 }'  partly optimized\n           pattern: optimized; action: post-retrieval\n           Action does more than print the line or its fields.\n           Try: Print only, e.g. `print $1, HOST`.\n      2  grep 'first row'                                    post-retrieval\n\n    Barrier: stage 1. Everything after it: post-retrieval.\n\n    $ explain cat /log/acme/api/main.log | grep -c boom\n      1  grep -c boom  post-retrieval\n           grep optimized with -i, -v, or no flags; not -c.\n           Try: Use `grep PATTERN | wc -l`: both optimized.\n\n    Barrier: stage 1. Everything after it: post-retrieval.\n\n    $ explain cat /log/acme/api/main.log | grep ERROR | wc -l\n    Every stage: optimized retrieval.\n\n  In a terminal the placements are colored: green for optimized retrieval,\n  yellow for partly optimized, red for post-retrieval.\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mSAVED CRONS\x1b[0m\n  A cron runs on every fire, so saving one explains it automatically. Nothing is\n  printed when the whole pipeline is optimized retrieval; otherwise the save\n  succeeds and the same report follows it.\n"

// ManLess — less (generated from docs/shell/man-less.md).
const ManLess = "LESS(1)                         Weft Commands                          LESS(1)\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mNAME\x1b[0m\n  less — page through output one screen at a time\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mSYNOPSIS\x1b[0m\n    <pipeline> | less\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mDESCRIPTION\x1b[0m\n  An in-process pager for weft. It runs inside the shell and behaves\n  the same in the web shell and the native CLI.\n\n  When a pipeline ends with | less the query runs to completion,\n  the result is loaded into the pager, and the shell shows\n  the pager until you press q.\n\n  Help and man pages auto-page when their output would overflow\n  the viewport — press q to return to the prompt.\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mNAVIGATION\x1b[0m\n    space, Page Down, f, Ctrl+F   forward one screen\n    b, Page Up, Ctrl+B            back one screen\n    j, Down                       forward one line\n    k, Up                         back one line\n    g, gg                         jump to top\n    G                             jump to bottom\n    q, Ctrl+C                     exit the pager\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mSEARCH\x1b[0m\n    /pattern    search forward (case-sensitive substring)\n    n           next match\n    N           previous match\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mSTATUS LINE\x1b[0m\n  Bottom row shows the source title on the left and a percent\n  position on the right. Incremental search and error messages\n  replace the status briefly.\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mSEE ALSO\x1b[0m\n  help, man alert, man range\n"

// ManPasswd — passwd (generated from docs/shell/man-passwd.md).
const ManPasswd = "PASSWD(5)                       Weft Commands                        PASSWD(5)\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mNAME\x1b[0m\n  passwd — the org team roster and its group-based permissions\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mSYNOPSIS\x1b[0m\n    cat /etc/passwd\n    adduser <email> [<group>]\n    deluser <email> [<group>]\n    groups [<email>]\n    id [<email>]\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mDESCRIPTION\x1b[0m\n  A weft org's members and their permissions are modeled as Unix-style\n  group membership. /etc/passwd is the read-only roster — one row per\n  member with their role, status, and groups:\n\n    USER             ROLE     STATUS    GROUPS\n    alice@acme.com   admin    active    binmgr  cronmgr  configmgr  nsmgr  payments  payments+tokens\n    bob@acme.com     member   active    binmgr  cronmgr  payments  payments+tokens\n    carol@acme.com   member   invited   —\n\n  Change the team with adduser and deluser (admin-only), or from\n  the dashboard's Members tab. An admin (sometimes\n  called the org owner) holds groups like any other member and is listed\n  with them.\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mGROUPS\x1b[0m\n  A group is one of these:\n\n  - binmgr / cronmgr / configmgr — edit access to /bin,\n    /cron.d, and /etc respectively.\n  - nsmgr — create and manage namespaces.\n  - <namespace> — read (query) the logs under /log/<namespace>.\n  - <namespace>+tokens — mint producer tokens for that namespace.\n    Independent of read: a member can have one, the other, or both.\n\n  The +tokens facet uses + because + is illegal in a namespace\n  name, so it can never collide with a real namespace.\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mCOMMANDS\x1b[0m\n  - adduser <email> — invite someone to the org. Sends an invite\n    email; they join when they accept (or next sign in).\n  - adduser <email> <group> — add a member to a group. If the email\n    isn't on the team yet, they're invited now (the email goes out) and\n    the grant applies after they accept, so re-run the command once\n    they've joined. If they were invited but haven't accepted, the\n    command returns an error.\n  - deluser <email> <group> — revoke a group membership.\n  - deluser <email> — remove a member from the org entirely.\n  - groups [<email>] — list a member's groups (yours if omitted).\n    Prints <email>: group group ...; an email not on the team gives\n    no such member: <email>.\n  - id [<email>] — show a member's role and groups, as\n    uid=<email> role=<role> groups=a,b.\n\n  Every command takes --help (prints usage, changes nothing). An unknown\n  flag, a first argument that isn't an email address, or extra arguments\n  is a usage error.\n\n  adduser/deluser are admin-only. Group grants apply once the\n  member has accepted their invite.\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mEXAMPLES\x1b[0m\n    adduser dana@acme.com                  # invite\n    adduser dana@acme.com payments         # let dana read payments logs\n    adduser dana@acme.com payments+tokens  # ...and mint producer tokens\n    adduser dana@acme.com cronmgr          # ...and edit /cron.d\n    deluser dana@acme.com payments         # revoke read\n    deluser dana@acme.com                  # remove from the org\n    groups dana@acme.com\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mSEE ALSO\x1b[0m\n  help builtins, help index\n"

// ManRange — range (generated from docs/shell/man-range.md).
const ManRange = "RANGE(1)                        Weft Commands                         RANGE(1)\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mNAME\x1b[0m\n  range — display or set the log query time range\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mSYNOPSIS\x1b[0m\n    range\n    range <start>..<end>\n    range <start>..\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mDESCRIPTION\x1b[0m\n  With no arguments, range displays the current query time window.\n\n  With a <start>..<end> argument, range sets the time window used\n  by all subsequent queries. If <end> is omitted, it defaults to\n  now. Relative specs are re-resolved against the server's clock on\n  every query — range 1d.. always means \"the last 24 hours\".\n\n  Each side of the range can be:\n\n  \x1b[38;5;208mABSOLUTE TIMESTAMP\x1b[0m\n  A calendar date or instant. Missing components default to the\n  start of the period: an omitted time means 00:00:00, an omitted\n  day means the 1st, an omitted month means January. An omitted\n  year defaults to the current year, so 01-15 in March means\n  current-year January.\n  Server-local timezone unless the literal carries one.\n\n    2026-04-12T10:30:00Z    full RFC3339 with timezone\n    2026-04-12T10:30:00     second resolution\n    2026-04-12T10:30        minute resolution\n    2026-04-12T10           hour resolution\n    2026-04-12              day resolution\n    2026-04                 month resolution\n    2026                    year resolution\n    04-12T10:30             current-year shorthand with time\n    04-12                   current-year shorthand, midnight\n\n  \x1b[38;5;208mRELATIVE DURATION\x1b[0m\n  Distance back from \"now\". Re-anchored against the server's clock\n  on every query, so a long-lived shell pane always queries a current\n  window. Units are m (minutes), h (hours) and d (days);\n  seconds, weeks and combined forms such as 1h30m are not accepted.\n\n    30m                     30 minutes ago\n    2h                      2 hours ago\n    7d                      7 days ago\n\n  \x1b[38;5;208mNAMED REFERENCES\x1b[0m\n    now                     current time\n    today                   midnight today\n    yesterday               midnight yesterday\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mEXAMPLES\x1b[0m\n    range                   show current range\n    range 2h..              last 2 hours\n    range 30m..             last 30 minutes\n    range 7d..              last 7 days\n    range 1h..10m           from 1 hour ago to 10 minutes ago\n    range yesterday..today  all of yesterday\n    range yesterday..       yesterday to now\n    range 04-01..05-14      current-year span, Apr 1 00:00 up to\n                            (excluding) May 14 00:00\n    range 05-14..           from May 14 midnight to now\n    range 05-14T09..05-14T17\n                            business hours on May 14 (current year)\n    range 2026-04-01..2026-04-12\n                            explicit-year date range\n    range 2026-04-12T10:00..2026-04-12T11:00\n                            specific hour\n"

// ManualAlertingStrategies — Alerting strategies (generated from docs/manual/alerting-strategies.md).
const ManualAlertingStrategies = "ALERTING-STRATEGIES(1)          Weft Commands           ALERTING-STRATEGIES(1)\n\n  This page is the catalogue of shapes an alert can take in weft —\n  single-cron scalar alerts, multi-row per-tag alerts, composite\n  alerts that fire on several conditions at once, and\n  aggregated-value alerts over a window. The alert builtin's grammar\n  lives in man alert; the weft::* family that backs predicates and\n  aggregates lives in Weft functions. This page is about which\n  shape fits which monitoring problem.\n\n  A cron run reads only the logs from its own schedule interval: a\n  */5 cron sees the last five minutes. The recipes here count with\n  grep ... | wc -l and do not limit rows with head -n or\n  tail -n: a line count caps the rows read, so on a busy interval it\n  truncates the window and the cron undercounts. (At the prompt, tail -n 500\n  | awk 'LEVEL==\"ERROR\"' | wc -l counts errors among the last 500 rows\n  only, and grep ERROR | tail -n 5 is the last five matches.) To try a\n  recipe interactively, set the window with range (see man range) and\n  run the pipeline without its alert stage, or with alert as the last\n  stage to see whether it fires.\n\n  Every example sends its alert to an alias with --notify=<alias>.\n  Aliases (ops, critical and so on) are defined in\n  /etc/notify.yaml; see Notifications.\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mThe four shapes\x1b[0m\n  \x1b[38;5;208m1. Single-cron scalar\x1b[0m\n  The most common form. The cron's pipeline ends in | alert <cond>;\n  each tick produces one numeric value that the alert evaluates.\n\n    # /cron.d/error-rate\n    # schedule: every 1m\n    cat /log/myco/prod/api/main.log | grep ERROR | wc -l | alert above 50 --notify=ops\n\n  Use this when \"is the metric above a known threshold?\" answers\n  your question and the metric is one number.\n\n  \x1b[38;5;208m2. Multi-row per-tag\x1b[0m\n  When a tick produces multiple values keyed by some dimension —\n  \"disk usage per host,\" \"error count per service\" — use the\n  -k/-t form of alert. Each row opens and closes incidents\n  independently against its tag.\n\n    # /cron.d/disk-per-host\n    # schedule: every 1m\n    cat /log/myco/prod/api/disk.log \\\n      | jq -r '\"\\($host) \\(.pct)\"' \\\n      | alert above 80 -k 2 -t 1 --notify=ops\n\n  -k 2 says \"field 2 holds the numeric value\"; -t 1 says \"field\n  1 names this row\" (the host). Each host opens and closes incidents\n  independently, giving one PagerDuty incident per affected host.\n\n  -k and -t also accept JSON paths, which skips the jq -r\n  text formatting. The host is a column of the row, not a field in its\n  JSON, so build the row with it, using jq -c so each row stays on one\n  line; multi-line jq output is read one line per row:\n\n    cat /log/myco/prod/api/disk.log \\\n      | jq -c '{host: $host, pct}' \\\n      | alert above 80 -k .pct -t .host --notify=ops\n\n  A single alert may not mix the two forms — both -k and -t must\n  be positional, or both must be .-prefixed paths.\n\n  The same shape works with any field of the row as the tag — swap\n  .host for .deployment or .path. The tag is one token, so to\n  combine fields use the positional form and join them without a\n  space: \"\\(.deployment)/\\(.host) \\(.pct)\" gives tags like\n  api/web-1.\n\n  \x1b[38;5;208m3. Composite (more than one condition)\x1b[0m\n  When the alert depends on more than one thing — \"page me when errors\n  are high and the canary stopped pinging\" — there are two ways to\n  combine conditions. Both end in a single pipeline whose last stage is\n  alert, because alert works only as the last (or only) command of a\n  script.\n\n  Over the cron's time range. Read every source the condition\n  needs, tally each leg in one awk pass, and print 1 when the whole\n  condition holds and 0 otherwise. Use this when the legs are visible\n  in the logs themselves:\n\n    # /cron.d/deploy-danger\n    # schedule: every 1m\n    cat /log/myco/prod/api/main.log /log/myco/prod/canary/main.log \\\n      | awk 'LEVEL==\"ERROR\"{e++} SERVICE==\"canary\"{c++}\n             END{print (e>50 && c+0==0) ? 1 : 0}' \\\n      | alert above 0 --notify=critical\n\n  e counts errors and c counts canary lines in the cron's interval;\n  c+0==0 is true when no canary line arrived at all. && is AND,\n  || is OR and ! is NOT inside the awk expression:\n  print (e>50 || p>0) ? 1 : 0 fires when either leg is high, and\n  print (e>50 && !m) ? 1 : 0 fires only when the m leg is zero (say,\n  a maintenance marker).\n\n  Across crons' history. When the legs are the stored results of other\n  crons, compose the weft::* predicates with bash && || ! in a\n  subshell, end it with an echo, and pipe that into alert. The\n  subshell prints fire only when the whole condition holds, and\n  alert's default any condition fires on any output:\n\n    # /cron.d/deploy-danger\n    # schedule: every 1m\n    (weft::is_above error-rate 50 && weft::is_absent canary-ping && echo fire) | alert --notify=critical\n\n  weft::is_above error-rate 50 && weft::is_absent canary-ping succeeds\n  (exit 0, the bash convention for success) when both predicates fire,\n  so the echo runs. weft::is_absent fires when the named cron has no\n  result: it doesn't exist, has never run, or its latest output is\n  empty (with a window, such as weft::is_absent canary-ping 10m: no result\n  landed in it). (A bad window, one over the 24h cap, or a history that can't\n  be read doesn't fire; in a bash predicate the reason is printed on\n  stderr.)\n\n  || (OR) and ! (NOT) work the same way:\n\n    (weft::is_above error-rate 50 || weft::is_above payment-fail 0 && echo fire) | alert --notify=critical\n\n    (! weft::is_absent maintenance-flag && weft::is_above error-rate 50 && echo fire) | alert --notify=critical   # fires only outside maintenance\n\n  Lua mirrors the bash predicates one-for-one, with native and or\n  not. Lua lives in a /bin/ script (crons are bash), which prints 1\n  or 0 and which the cron pipes into alert above 0. Use Lua when the\n  chain has more than two clauses, when the predicate uses computed values,\n  or when you want a meaningful debug print:\n\n    #!/usr/bin/env lua\n    -- /bin/deploy_danger\n    local rate = 0\n    if not weft.is_absent(\"error-rate\") then\n      rate = tonumber(weft.latest(\"error-rate\")) or 0\n    end\n    local canary_dead = weft.is_absent(\"canary-ping\")\n    local in_maintenance = not weft.is_absent(\"maintenance-flag\")\n\n    if rate > 50 and canary_dead and not in_maintenance then\n      io.stderr:write(string.format(\"composite fire: rate=%.1f canary_dead=%s\\n\",\n        rate, tostring(canary_dead)))\n      print(1)\n    else\n      print(0)\n    end\n\n    # /cron.d/deploy-danger\n    # schedule: every 1m\n    deploy_danger | alert above 0 --notify=critical\n\n  \x1b[38;5;208m4. Aggregated-value (windowed) alerts\x1b[0m\n  When the question is \"what does the value look like over a window,\"\n  use a weft::* aggregate in the cron body and alert against its\n  stdout:\n\n    # /cron.d/error-rate-hourly\n    # schedule: every 5m\n    echo $(weft::avg error-rate 1h) | alert above 50 --notify=ops\n\n  Or compose with the stdin comparators when chaining into a\n  composite:\n\n    (weft::avg error-rate 5m | weft::is_gt 50 && weft::is_absent canary-ping && echo fire) | alert --notify=critical\n\n  The aggregates' window caps (24h for avg/min/max/etc., 14d for\n  endpoint funcs) limit how far back the rolling window can reach.\n  For longer-horizon comparisons, write a cron that itself runs an\n  aggregate every 5 minutes — its stored history then is the\n  longer-horizon series.\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mTrend, anomaly and threshold conditions\x1b[0m\n  \x1b[38;5;8mWant to fire when...                    Use\x1b[0m\n  \x1b[38;5;8m--------------------------------------  --------------------------------------\x1b[0m\n  Value crosses a known threshold         alert above / alert below (or\n                                          weft::is_above / weft::is_below in a\n                                          composite)\n  Value differs from the previous tick    weft::has_changed\n  at all\n  No result came in                       weft::is_absent\n  Value rose/fell by N% vs recent         alert rising / alert falling (or\n  baseline                                weft::is_rising / weft::is_falling)\n  Value is unusual for this cron's        weft::is_outlier\n  recent history\n  Value is unusual for this cron at this  weft::is_seasonal\n  time of day/week\n\n  The functions take different arguments:\n\n  - weft::is_rising <cron> [<pct>] [<window>], and weft::is_falling\n    likewise. <pct> is a bare number such as 50; it takes no % suffix.\n  - weft::is_outlier <cron> <n_stddev> <window>\n  - weft::is_seasonal <cron> <n_stddev> <period> <window>\n\n  Windows and periods are Go durations plus d (24 hours) and w (7\n  days), and they combine: 30m, 1h, 1d, 1w, 1d12h. See Weft\n  functions for the caps on each function.\n\n  Use is_outlier when \"what's normal\" doesn't depend on the clock —\n  host memory, queue depth, error rate of a steady-state service. Use\n  is_seasonal when traffic has weekly/daily cycles — a Tuesday-2pm\n  threshold under-pages on Saturday at 3am and over-pages at the\n  nightly batch job.\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mAbsent and zero values\x1b[0m\n  A cron with no usable value and a value of zero are different cases. In\n  bash, weft::latest of a cron that doesn't exist is an error; in Lua, tonumber(\"\") returns nil and arithmetic against\n  nil errors. Always check for absence and guard the conversion:\n\n    local v = 0   -- treat absent as zero\n    if not weft.is_absent(\"foo\") then\n      v = tonumber(weft.latest(\"foo\")) or 0\n    end\n\n  In bash, predicate builtins handle this for you — weft::is_above\n  missing 50 exits 1 (no fire).\n\n  To distinguish absent from zero, use\n  weft::is_absent explicitly in the chain:\n\n    weft::is_absent error-rate    # exit 0 (fires) when error-rate has no result, or doesn't exist\n    # ...whereas...\n    weft::is_above error-rate 0   # fires only when there IS a result AND it's > 0\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mDebouncing flaps\x1b[0m\n  Composite conditions tend to flap — each leg individually crosses\n  back and forth across its threshold, and the composite pops whenever\n  any pair simultaneously trips. The alert --duration <dur>\n  flag handles this: it requires the trigger to hold for the entire\n  window before firing. Once the composite output is a scalar (1 or 0\n  piped through alert), --duration works the same as for any other\n  alert. (--duration takes s, m and h; 1d is rejected.)\n\n    (weft::is_above error-rate 50 && weft::is_absent canary-ping && echo 1 || echo 0) | alert above 0 --duration 5m --notify=critical\n\n  Five minutes of sustained composite-true before the page.\n\n  The same applies to multi-row alerts — --duration is per-tag, so\n  alert above 80 -k 2 -t 1 --duration 5m requires each host's\n  five-minute window to clear the threshold before that host's\n  incident opens.\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mAnti-patterns\x1b[0m\n  - Don't fan out alerts from inside the chain. A predicate\n    builtin is a test and has no side effects. To page multiple\n    aliases from one cron, write multiple /cron.d/ entries or use\n    one notify.yaml alias that fans out to multiple destinations.\n  - Don't put weft::* predicates on the right side of |. They\n    read stored state. For stdin-driven comparisons, pipe values\n    through weft::is_gt / weft::is_lt.\n  - Don't compose state-mutating commands in the chain. Keep the\n    predicate composition side-effect-free: join weft::* predicates\n    with &&, || and !, and nothing else. Hanging an unrelated\n    command off the chain couples alerting to unrelated I/O and makes\n    failure modes harder to reason about.\n  - Don't reuse a single-row alert for multi-row data. Multiple\n    rows on stdin without -k/-t collapse into one scalar, which\n    the alert will reject as non-numeric. Either reduce upstream with\n    wc -l or awk (single-row), or add -k/-t (multi-row).\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mSee also\x1b[0m\n  - Weft functions — full reference for the weft::* value funcs,\n    predicates, and stdin comparators.\n  - Crons — scheduled pipelines and incident lifecycle.\n  - man alert — the alert builtin's full grammar.\n  - Notifications — alias and destination configuration.\n"

// ManualConnectCloudwatch — Connect a CloudWatch source (generated from docs/manual/cloudwatch.md).
const ManualConnectCloudwatch = "CONNECT-CLOUDWATCH(1)           Weft Commands            CONNECT-CLOUDWATCH(1)\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mConnect a CloudWatch source\x1b[0m\n  If your logs already land in AWS CloudWatch Logs — Lambda, ECS/Fargate,\n  API Gateway, RDS, or anything else that writes to a log group — weft can\n  ingest them with no agent. You deploy a small CloudFormation\n  stack in your own AWS account that forwards a CloudWatch Logs subscription\n  filter through a Kinesis Data Firehose delivery stream to weft.\n  Firehose pushes events to weft as they arrive.\n\n  This source type has nothing to install and nothing to run. Latency is\n  CloudWatch's subscription-filter delivery plus Firehose's buffering (roughly\n  a minute at the lowest buffering setting the CloudFormation template uses).\n  For sub-second tailing, install weftd.\n\n  \x1b[38;5;208m1. Register the source\x1b[0m\n  Use the weft CLI to tell weft which log group maps to which service\n  (the service half of ns/svc becomes the <service> path segment; see\n  Namespaces).\n  Run this from any machine with the weft binary installed and a valid\n  login.\n\n    weft source add myco/my-app \\\n      --log-group /aws/lambda/my-fn \\\n      --region us-east-2\n\n  This records the mapping. Ingestion starts after the later steps. The source\n  shows up as pending in weft source list (or run weft source pending):\n\n    weft source list\n\n  \x1b[38;5;208m2. Deploy the CloudFormation stack\x1b[0m\n  In the AWS account that owns the log group, deploy the Firehose stack.\n  It provisions the Firehose delivery stream, the IAM roles CloudWatch Logs\n  needs to write to it, the subscription filter on your log group(s), and a\n  required S3 bucket for any events Firehose can't deliver (30-day expiry).\n\n    aws cloudformation create-stack \\\n      --stack-name weft-cloudwatch-firehose \\\n      --template-url https://weft-public.s3.amazonaws.com/cfn/weft-cloudwatch-firehose.yaml \\\n      --parameters ParameterKey=WeftToken,ParameterValue=<your-weft-namespace-token> \\\n                   ParameterKey=LogGroupNames,ParameterValue=\"/aws/lambda/my-fn\" \\\n      --capabilities CAPABILITY_IAM\n\n  WeftToken is a namespace token (create one (man namespaces)\n  for a namespace that already exists). It authenticates Firehose's deliveries\n  to weft the same way any other push source authenticates. LogGroupNames is\n  a comma-separated list, so one stack can subscribe several log groups in the\n  same account/region.\n\n  To add or remove log groups, update the stack's LogGroupNames parameter and\n  redeploy. The Firehose stream and IAM roles stay unchanged.\n\n  \x1b[38;5;208m3. Approve a schema\x1b[0m\n  Once your log group starts emitting events, weft accumulates a sample from\n  that traffic and the source goes from sampling to pending; approve it as\n  described in Connect a log source (man overview):\n\n    weft source configure myco/my-app\n\n  The wizard detects CloudWatch-flavored text\n  (2026-04-18 14:32:01 INFO {\"user\":\"alice\"}) and JSON Lambda logs\n  automatically, so you usually confirm its suggestions.\n\n    # And in the shell\n    cat /log/myco/my-app/main.log | head\n\n  That path reads the service across every env. If the token you deployed\n  carries an env, the rows live under /log/myco/<env>/my-app/main.log; see\n  Paths (man introduction).\n\n  \x1b[38;5;208mTroubleshooting\x1b[0m\n  - No rows appearing — check the Firehose delivery stream's own\n    CloudWatch log group (created by the stack, separate from your app's\n    log group) for delivery errors; a common cause is the WeftToken\n    parameter not matching a live namespace token. Also confirm the\n    subscription filter attached — CloudWatch allows at most 2\n    subscription filters per log group, so if you already forward this log\n    group elsewhere (Datadog, Splunk) you may be out of slots.\n  - Nothing to configure yet — a low-traffic log group can take a while to\n    accumulate a sample; weft source pending --watch shows its progress.\n  - Rows appearing but fields look wrong — your schema's\n    timestamp_format is off. Check against Go's reference time\n    (Mon Jan 2 15:04:05 MST 2006), then resample and re-run the wizard (see\n    Connect a log source (man overview)).\n"

// ManualCommands — Weft's own commands (generated from docs/manual/commands.md).
const ManualCommands = "COMMANDS(1)                     Weft Commands                      COMMANDS(1)\n\n  Most of the shell is the Unix you know: cat, grep, awk, sed, jq,\n  sort and the rest behave as they do in bash (see Builtins). A few\n  commands exist only in weft, because they act on things a Unix shell doesn't\n  have: the query window, your crons, alerts, dashboards, your team. Each has a\n  reference page, shown with man <name> in the shell and listed below.\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mQuerying\x1b[0m\n  \x1b[38;5;8mCommand                 What it does                               Reference\x1b[0m\n  \x1b[38;5;8m----------------------  -----------------------------------------  -----------\x1b[0m\n  range [<start>..<end>]  Show or set the time window every read     man range\n                          uses. Relative specs like 1d.. are\n                          re-anchored on every query.\n  explain <pipeline>      Say how much of a one-liner is optimized   man explain\n                          retrieval and what would speed it up.\n                          Runs nothing.\n\n  \x1b[38;5;208mMaking a pipeline faster\x1b[0m\n  A pipeline is fastest when its stages are part of the optimized retrieval:\n  they narrow and reshape rows as the logs are fetched, so only the result comes\n  back. Any other stage runs post-retrieval, on every row that came back.\n  explain marks each stage green (optimized retrieval), yellow (partly\n  optimized) or red (post-retrieval), names the first one that isn't fully\n  optimized (the barrier), and says what to change:\n\n    $ explain cat /log/acme/api/main.log | grep -c boom\n      1  grep -c boom  post-retrieval\n           grep optimized with -i, -v, or no flags; not -c.\n           Try: Use `grep PATTERN | wc -l`: both optimized.\n\n    Barrier: stage 1. Everything after it: post-retrieval.\n\n  When a pipeline is already entirely optimized, explain says so. Saving a\n  cron runs the same check and prints the report after the save, only when\n  something isn't optimized.\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mOutput\x1b[0m\n  \x1b[38;5;8mCommand  What it does                                                Reference\x1b[0m\n  \x1b[38;5;8m-------  ----------------------------------------------------------  ---------\x1b[0m\n  chart    Render a pipeline's output, or a cron's history, as a       man chart\n           chart in the terminal.\n  less     Page output. Long output pages automatically; press q to    man less\n           return.\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mAlerting and crons\x1b[0m\n  \x1b[38;5;8mCommand                     What it does                 Reference\x1b[0m\n  \x1b[38;5;8m--------------------------  ---------------------------  ---------------------\x1b[0m\n  alert                       Fire a notification from a   man alert, man notify\n                              pipeline's final number.\n  weft::latest, weft::avg,    Read and reason about your   man weft-funcs\n  weft::max, ...              own cron history from\n                              inside a pipeline.\n  dashboard                   Open a dashboard; the YAML   man dashboard\n                              grammar and tile kinds.\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mSharing and history\x1b[0m\n  \x1b[38;5;8mCommand    What it does                             Reference\x1b[0m\n  \x1b[38;5;8m---------  ---------------------------------------  --------------------------\x1b[0m\n  git        Version history for /bin, /cron.d and    man git\n             /etc: log, show, diff, blame, restore,\n             revert.\n  clipboard  Copy a short, shareable link to a /bin   man clipboard, man sharing\n             script.\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mTeam\x1b[0m\n  \x1b[38;5;8mCommand                     What it does                 Reference\x1b[0m\n  \x1b[38;5;8m--------------------------  ---------------------------  ---------------------\x1b[0m\n  adduser, deluser, groups,   Manage org members and       man passwd, man teams\n  id                          groups, not\n                              operating-system accounts.\n                              adduser <email> sends an\n                              invite.\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mFinding help\x1b[0m\n  help lists the shell's topics, help builtins lists every command, and\n  man with no argument lists the manual. <command> --help shows the same\n  page as man <command>.\n"

// ManualOverview — Overview (generated from docs/manual/connect-source.md).
const ManualOverview = "OVERVIEW(1)                     Weft Commands                      OVERVIEW(1)\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mConnect a log source\x1b[0m\n  The process for connecting a log source to weft is mostly the same regardless\n  of whether you're using our own agent, pushing batches over HTTPS, or streaming\n  syslog over TCP:\n\n  1. Create a token in the namespace of your choosing as described in Namespaces\n     & tokens (man namespaces);\n  2. Feed that token to your log shipping mechanism;\n  3. Configure a schema to extract useful bits of information from your log lines\n     (optional for source types we don't control; with our own agent, we enforce\n     this part).\n\n  \x1b[38;5;208mSupported producers\x1b[0m\n  - weftd (man weftd) is our homegrown log shipping agent. It's the fastest\n    option and has a few more bells and whistles but you have to be able to\n    actually run it -- not possible in every environment.\n  - Cloudwatch+Kinesis (man connect-cloudwatch) -- our recommended path for monitoring\n    AWS Lambdas.\n  - Heroku (man heroku) -- hey, Heroku is actually still in business, and we've\n    still got some applications running there, so we got to support!\n  - Render (man connect-render) -- our first foray into the new batch of Heroku successors.\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mSampling logs for a schema\x1b[0m\n  Once a source starts producing lines, you'll see it in the \"sources\" under its\n  respective namespace. It will start off in a \"sampling\" state -- we capture a\n  50-line chunk to help to define a schema for that source. You can watch that\n  progress in the browser or with weft source pending --watch.\n\n  When using weftd, you're forced to define a schema for your log lines, unless\n  the lines were written by a weft SDK to a log file (man weftd):\n  then timestamp, level, trace ID and spans come from each line's header and\n  there is nothing to define. Though you don't have to define a schema for other\n  producers, we highly recommend it.\n  Having a means of plucking metavariables (man metavars) out of the logs lets us\n  make lookups on those values nice and snappy.\n\n  Once you've transtioned from \"sampling\" to \"pending,\" you can view our\n  best guess about your sample in the browser or on the CLI with weft source\n  configure. A little wizard will walk you through our guesses for each of the\n  values we want to extract and you can approve, reject, etc.\n\n  You can resample at any time by clicking \"resample\" or running weft source\n  resample.\n"

// ManualCrons — Crons (generated from docs/manual/crons.md).
const ManualCrons = "CRONS(1)                        Weft Commands                         CRONS(1)\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mSet up a cron\x1b[0m\n  Crons are saved pipelines that run on a schedule. Create one from\n  the shell:\n\n    # Open the cron editor\n    vi /cron.d/error-count\n\n    # Cron file format: a '# schedule:' directive plus a pipeline.\n    # schedule: */5 * * * *\n    cat /log/myco/prod/api/main.log | grep ERROR | wc -l | alert above 0 --notify=ops\n\n  The schedule is either a five-field expression (*/5 * * * *) or\n  every <duration> (# schedule: every 5m). Each run reads the\n  logs from its own schedule interval: a */5 cron sees the last five\n  minutes, so the pipeline above counts that interval's errors with no\n  row limit. A cron must not use head -n or tail -n: a line count caps\n  the rows read, and on a busy interval it silently cuts the window short, so\n  the cron undercounts. To try a cron's pipeline at the\n  prompt first, set the window with range (see man range).\n\n  The pipeline's stdout is captured as the cron result — numeric\n  values are graphed on the dashboard as sparklines. Alerts are\n  dispatched by the alert stage (see Alerting strategies for\n  the catalogue of alert shapes, plus man alert for the builtin's\n  grammar).\n\n  View and manage crons from the Dashboard.\n\n  \x1b[38;5;208mAccessing past results\x1b[0m\n  Every cron run is stored. The weft::* family pulls summaries out\n  of that history (aggregates like weft::avg, point reads like\n  weft::latest) and a parallel predicate family (weft::is_above\n  etc.) exits 0 (fire) or 1 (don't fire) for use in composite alerts. They're\n  available at the shell prompt, inside /bin/ scripts, and as\n  pipeline stages in other crons:\n\n    # Interactively, at the prompt\n    weft::latest error-rate\n\n    # Chained through a pipeline\n    echo $(weft::avg error-rate 1h) | alert above 50 --notify=ops\n\n  From a Lua /bin/ script the return type is a number:\n\n    local rate = weft.avg(\"error-rate\", \"1h\")\n\n  Windows are Go durations plus d (a day) and w (a week), and they\n  combine: 30m, 1h, 1d, 1h30m. Aggregates reach back at most 24\n  hours (1d).\n\n  The full reference (value funcs, predicates, stdin comparators,\n  caps, numeric-parsing rules) is in Weft functions. Patterns\n  that combine these into alerts of various shapes — multi-row\n  per-tag, composite, anomaly-based — live in Alerting strategies.\n\n  Tier limits (number of crons and minimum schedule interval) vary by\n  plan — see the Account page for your current caps, or run\n  help cron in the shell.\n\n  \x1b[38;5;208mCron version history\x1b[0m\n  Every save to /cron.d/<name> is captured. After :w, the shell\n  prompts for an optional commit message; git log -- /cron.d/<name>\n  lists past versions, and git restore --source=HEAD~2 /cron.d/<name>\n  makes an old version current again by saving it as a new version. The\n  live version plus the five before it are kept. See git for the full\n  surface.\n"

// ManualDashboards — Dashboards (generated from docs/manual/dashboards.md).
const ManualDashboards = "DASHBOARDS(1)                   Weft Commands                    DASHBOARDS(1)\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mWhat dashboards are\x1b[0m\n  A dashboard is a named view that arranges existing crons into tiles.\n  Each tile reads from a cron. The cron's schedule and history\n  define what the tile shows; the dashboard arranges the tiles into a\n  layout.\n\n  Dashboards live as YAML files under /etc/dashboards/. Create one from\n  the shell:\n\n    vi /etc/dashboards/prod.yaml\n\n  Writing under /etc requires the configmgr group.\n\n  Each customer can have any number of dashboards, distinguished by name.\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mYAML grammar\x1b[0m\n    title: Production\n    sections:\n      - title: Errors\n        tiles:\n          - kind: sparkline\n            cron: error-rate-hourly\n            size: md\n\n          - kind: table\n            cron: top-errors-hourly\n            columns: [host, count]\n            size: lg\n\n      - title: Capacity\n        tiles:\n          - kind: counter\n            cron: pending-jobs\n            format: \"%d jobs\"\n            threshold: { warn: 100, crit: 500 }\n            size: sm\n\n          - kind: gauge\n            cron: cpu-usage\n            threshold: { warn: 70, crit: 90, max: 100 }\n            size: md\n\n  Each tile's cron: key names a cron. Create the crons first; a dashboard that references an unknown cron is rejected.\n\n  Top-level fields:\n\n  \x1b[38;5;8mField     Required  Notes\x1b[0m\n  \x1b[38;5;8m--------  --------  ---------------------------------------------------\x1b[0m\n  title     no        Heading at the top of the dashboard.\n  sections  yes\\*     List of grouped tile sections.\n  tiles     yes\\*     Shorthand: a flat tile list = one untitled section.\n\n  \\* Use one of sections: or top-level tiles:.\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mTile kinds\x1b[0m\n  \x1b[38;5;8mKind       Body                                                Required fields\x1b[0m\n  \x1b[38;5;8m---------  --------------------------------------------------  ---------------\x1b[0m\n  sparkline  Time-series glyph from the cron's recent results.   cron\n  counter    Latest numeric value.                               cron\n  gauge      Bar from 0 to threshold.max (default 100).          cron\n  table      Multi-row stdout parsed as whitespace-split         cron, columns\n             columns.\n\n  Tile fields:\n\n  \x1b[38;5;8mField      Applies to                 Notes\x1b[0m\n  \x1b[38;5;8m---------  -------------------------  ----------------------------------------\x1b[0m\n  kind       all                        One of sparkline, counter, gauge, table.\n  cron       all                        Name of an existing cron (must exist\n                                        when the file is saved).\n  title      all                        Optional label for the tile.\n  size       all                        sm, md, or lg. Default md. Layout hint.\n  format     counter                    Optional. Text with exactly one %d\n                                        (rounded integer) or %.2f (any decimals,\n                                        %.Nf); %% is a literal %. E.g. \"%d ms\",\n                                        \"%.1f%%\".\n  threshold  counter, gauge, sparkline  warn, crit numeric cutoffs; max (gauge)\n                                        sets full scale.\n  columns    table                      Header labels; columns parse as\n                                        whitespace-split fields.\n  tag, y     sparkline                  Multi-line mode — see below. Must be\n                                        set together.\n\n  A field on a kind it does not apply to is rejected when the file is saved\n  (format on a gauge, columns on a counter, threshold on a table).\n\n  \x1b[38;5;208mMulti-line sparklines (tag:)\x1b[0m\n  A sparkline plots one scalar per cron run (the cron's last\n  whitespace field). When a cron's stdout is multiple JSON rows\n  per run — one per tagged entity, e.g. one row per instance — set tag:\n  (which field identifies the row) and y: (which field to plot) to draw\n  one line per distinct tag value:\n\n    - kind: sparkline\n      cron: coordinator-mem-pct\n      tag: .coordinator_id\n      y: .mem_pct\n      size: lg\n\n  Each row's x value is its run's timestamp from the cron run history. Lines\n  get a legend and a distinct color per tag; tag:/y: are rejected on\n  any kind other than sparkline.\n\n  The size: field is a layout hint. Each renderer\n  (browser / terminal) maps the hint onto its own viewport. On a wide\n  browser an lg tile spans the full row; on a narrow terminal it falls\n  back to single-column.\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mThreshold colors\x1b[0m\n  counter, gauge, and sparkline accept a threshold: map:\n\n    threshold: { warn: 70, crit: 90 }\n\n  Values at or above crit render in red; values at or above warn (and\n  below crit) render in yellow. gauge additionally uses\n  threshold.max to pick its full-scale value (default 100).\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mSections\x1b[0m\n  Each section gets its own heading and grid. Sections split a\n  dashboard into groups such as \"Errors\", \"Capacity\" and \"Throughput\".\n\n  For a dashboard without sections, use a flat top-level tiles: list:\n\n    title: Quick view\n    tiles:\n      - kind: counter\n        cron: pending-jobs\n      - kind: sparkline\n        cron: error-rate\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mLayout\x1b[0m\n  Dashboards describe layout intent. The renderer\n  flows tiles row-major and chooses how many fit per row based on the\n  viewport and each tile's size: hint.\n\n  - Browser: CSS grid with 6 tracks on wide screens, collapsing to 2\n    on tablet and 1 on phone. sm spans 2 tracks, md spans 3, lg\n    spans 6.\n  - Terminal: greedy row packing using kind-specific minimum widths.\n    Below ~60 columns the layout collapses to one tile per row.\n\n  Tiles are placed by size hint and YAML order. The order\n  in YAML is the order on screen.\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mNavigation\x1b[0m\n  Tile headers link to the cron's detail page (/cron/<name>) so\n  clicking a tile takes you to its full history with zoom, range\n  selection, and per-tag legend.\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mDeletion\x1b[0m\n  Remove a dashboard from the shell:\n\n    rm /etc/dashboards/old.yaml\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mValidation\x1b[0m\n  Saving a dashboard checks the YAML parses, every kind: is recognized,\n  every size: is one of sm / md / lg, every table tile has\n  columns:, and every referenced cron exists. A bad file is rejected\n  at write time — your previous version stays intact.\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mAuthoring via the MCP server\x1b[0m\n  Connect Claude Code (or any MCP host) to the weft MCP server and the\n  model can stage dashboards through the same review-and-apply pipeline\n  as scripts and crons:\n\n  \x1b[38;5;8mTool                    Effect\x1b[0m\n  \x1b[38;5;8m----------------------  ------------------------------------------------------\x1b[0m\n  weft.dashboard_propose  Stage a new or updated dashboard. Validates YAML +\n                          cron refs. Returns a unified diff against any existing\n                          version.\n  weft.proposal_delete    Stage a delete with kind: dashboard, name: <n>.\n  weft.proposals_apply    Commit one or more staged proposals after human\n                          review.\n\n  Cron references are checked against the live cron list; a dashboard\n  that names an unknown cron cannot be staged.\n"

// ManualGit — git — version history for /bin, /cron.d and /etc (generated from docs/manual/git.md).
const ManualGit = "GIT(1)                          Weft Commands                           GIT(1)\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mVersion history with git\x1b[0m\n  Every save to a script in /bin, a cron in /cron.d, a dashboard\n  in /etc/dashboards or /etc/notify.yaml is captured in a short,\n  browseable history. The git command shows earlier versions and\n  restores them.\n\n  git is a subset of git. Supported commands behave and print\n  the way git does: git log, git show, git diff, git blame,\n  git restore. git --help lists what is supported. Unsupported\n  commands are refused by name, and unsupported flags are refused with\n  fatal: unrecognized argument (the supported flags are listed under\n  git log):\n\n    weft> git rebase -i HEAD~3\n    git: 'rebase' is a git command, but weft doesn't support it\n\n  A misspelled command reads as a typo:\n\n    weft> git rebsae\n    git: 'rebsae' is not a git command. See 'git --help'.\n\n  There is one branch, main. Every save is a commit on it, in the\n  order the saves happened, across your whole org. There is no\n  staging area: a write is the commit. git add, merging, rebasing and\n  remotes do not exist.\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mWhat's tracked\x1b[0m\n  \x1b[38;5;8mPath                         Tracked?\x1b[0m\n  \x1b[38;5;8m---------------------------  -------------------------------------------------\x1b[0m\n  /bin/<name>                  ✓ — saves to scripts produce commits\n  /cron.d/<name>               ✓ — saves to crons produce commits\n  /etc/dashboards/<name>.yaml  ✓ — saves to dashboards produce commits\n  /etc/notify.yaml             ✓ — saves to the alert aliases produce\n                               commits\n  /etc/passwd, /etc/usage      ✗ — generated\n  /log/...                     ✗\n  /tmp/...                     ✗\n\n  The repository root is /, and tracked paths are bin/<name>,\n  cron.d/<name>, etc/dashboards/<name>.yaml and etc/notify.yaml.\n  Write a path any way git accepts it: /bin/scan,\n  bin/scan, or scan after cd /bin (after <rev>: use the\n  repo-relative form, bin/scan). A path outside the tracked set is refused: fatal: '/tmp/x' is outside the\n  repository (only /bin, /cron.d, /etc/dashboards and /etc/notify.yaml are tracked).\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mRevisions\x1b[0m\n  HEAD is the org's latest change. Use a path to narrow to a file (git log -- /bin/scan). Revisions work as in\n  git:\n\n  - HEAD, @, and the short hash git log prints (4 or more\n    characters).\n  - HEAD~N — N saves back. HEAD^ is HEAD~1. (HEAD^2 is the\n    second parent, as in git; this history has none, so it is an\n    error.)\n  - <a>..<b> — the commits in <b> that aren't in <a>.\n  - <rev>:<path> — a file's content as of a revision.\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mThe five-version cap\x1b[0m\n  We keep each file's latest six versions (the live one plus the\n  five before it). Older versions are deleted.\n\n  The oldest version still kept for a file is shown the way a\n  shallow clone shows its edge: git log marks it grafted, its\n  patch shows the file as newly added, git blame prefixes its\n  lines with ^, and revisions older than that don't resolve\n  (fatal: bad revision). The cutoff is per file.\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mCommands\x1b[0m\n  \x1b[38;5;208mgit log\x1b[0m\n    weft> git log --oneline\n    a4fb0c2 (HEAD -> main) fix the awk filter\n    3d2e91f tighten error pattern\n    c01b745 Update cron.d/errors\n    1e8a02c initial pass\n\n  git log is the org's combined history; git log -- /bin/scan is\n  one file's. It takes --oneline, -p, --stat, -n N/-N,\n  --author=, --grep=, -S<text> (commits that added or removed\n  the text), --since=/--until= (\"2 days ago\", 2026-10-01),\n  --decorate/--no-decorate, --all, and a <rev> or\n  <a>..<b>. Anything else is refused:\n  fatal: unrecognized argument: --bogus.\n\n  A save with no message gets a generated one — Create bin/scan,\n  Update bin/scan, Delete bin/scan — because git requires a\n  message.\n\n  \x1b[38;5;208mgit show\x1b[0m\n    weft> git show HEAD~2:bin/scan\n    cat /log/myco/api/main.log | grep ERROR | wc -l\n\n  git show <rev> prints a commit and its patch;\n  git show <rev>:<path> prints the file as it was then. Crons show\n  their # schedule: line first, so a version reads as a complete\n  cron file.\n\n  \x1b[38;5;208mgit diff\x1b[0m\n    weft> git diff HEAD~2 HEAD -- /bin/scan\n\n  git diff <a> <b> and git diff <a>..<b> compare two revisions,\n  optionally limited to paths after --. Plain git diff prints\n  nothing because the live file is HEAD.\n\n  \x1b[38;5;208mgit status\x1b[0m\n  Always On branch main / nothing to commit, working tree clean,\n  because the live file is HEAD.\n\n  \x1b[38;5;208mgit blame <path>\x1b[0m\n  Who last changed each line of a script or cron:\n\n    weft> git blame /bin/scan\n    ^1e8a02c (Eli Bergstrom 2026-04-22 17:33:12 +0000 1) cat /log/myco/api/main.log\n    a4fb0c2f (Dana Okafor 2026-04-25 09:42:01 +0000 2) | awk 'LEVEL == \"ERROR\"'\n\n  A ^ marks a line that comes from the oldest version still kept (the\n  file's first version, or the edge of the cap).\n\n  \x1b[38;5;208mgit restore, git checkout <rev> -- <path>\x1b[0m\n  Make an older version current again:\n\n    weft> git restore --source=HEAD~3 /bin/scan\n    weft> git checkout HEAD~3 -- /bin/scan        # same thing, older spelling\n\n  This writes the old content as a new commit (Restore bin/scan\n  from 0174c77) — history is never rewritten — through the same path as vi: your permissions, the\n  Lua/bash classification, and (for crons) your plan's schedule\n  limits all apply. It also brings back a script that was deleted,\n  if its history is still there. git checkout <rev> without --\n  is refused.\n\n  \x1b[38;5;208mgit revert <rev>\x1b[0m\n  Undo one commit with a new commit. If the file changed again since\n  that commit, git's conflict is reported and nothing is written:\n\n    error: could not revert 3d2e91f... tighten error pattern\n    hint: bin/scan has changed since that commit, so the revert would conflict; nothing was written\n\n  \x1b[38;5;208mgit commit --amend -m <message>\x1b[0m\n  Rewords the latest commit. You can reword only a file's current\n  version, only one you wrote yourself, and only with permission to\n  edit that file — so you can't reword a teammate's save that landed\n  after yours. A plain git commit reports nothing to commit.\n\n  \x1b[38;5;208mgit rev-parse\x1b[0m\n  git rev-parse HEAD, --short, --abbrev-ref HEAD, --verify\n  — handy inside /bin scripts.\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mColour and pipes\x1b[0m\n  Output that goes straight to your terminal is coloured. Output\n  headed into a pipe or a redirect is plain text, so\n  git log -p | grep foo and git show HEAD:bin/scan > /tmp/scan\n  work as in a shell. --color=always or --color=never overrides.\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mCommit messages at save time\x1b[0m\n  Right after a successful save in vi of a file git tracks (a script,\n  cron, dashboard or notify.yaml), the shell drops you into a\n  follow-up vi buffer pre-filled with a comment-prefixed prompt:\n\n    # Commit message for /bin/scan.\n    # One or more lines; the first line is the subject shown in `git log`.\n    # Save & exit (:wq) to record. Quit without saving (:q!) to skip.\n\n    █\n\n  Type a message, :wq records it. :q! skips it. Either way you\n  end up back at the shell prompt. Comment lines (#-prefixed) and\n  blank lines are stripped; an entirely-comment buffer records no\n  message. To add one later, use git commit --amend -m.\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mDeleting and bringing back\x1b[0m\n  Deleting a script or cron shows up as a Delete bin/<name>\n  commit, and git restore --source=<rev> <path> brings it back\n  with its history intact. Saving a new file over a deleted name does\n  the same thing: the deletion stays in the log, followed by the new\n  save.\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mPermissions\x1b[0m\n  git log, show, diff, status, blame and rev-parse are\n  read-only — the same access as cat /bin/foo or cat /cron.d/foo.\n\n  git restore, checkout -- <path>, revert and commit --amend\n  are writes. They need the same group as editing the file does:\n  binmgr for /bin, cronmgr for /cron.d, configmgr for /etc.\n  Restoring an old dashboard still validates it: if it refers to a cron\n  that has since been deleted, the restore is refused.\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mUnsupported commands\x1b[0m\n  - Working tree and staging. add, rm, mv, stash,\n    reset, clean are refused (is a git command, but weft doesn't\n    support it); every write is already a commit.\n  - Branches, merges and rebases. History is one linear sequence; these are refused.\n  - Remotes. clone, fetch, pull, push are refused.\n  - /tmp, /etc/passwd and /etc/usage. These keep no history.\n    /tmp is ephemeral; the other two are generated from group\n    membership and metering. Change the team with adduser / deluser.\n  - Slack setup. Only the alias file /etc/notify.yaml is versioned;\n    connecting Slack creates no commit.\n  - Patterns are RE2. --author and --grep use Go\n    regular expressions, so +, ? and | are operators.\n"

// ManualHeroku — Heroku (generated from docs/manual/heroku.md).
const ManualHeroku = "HEROKU(1)                       Weft Commands                        HEROKU(1)\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mConnecting a Heroku app\x1b[0m\n  Weft accepts logs directly from Heroku's HTTPS log-drain protocol. This page\n  covers the Heroku-specific parts; for an overview, see Connect a log\n  source (man overview).\n\n  \x1b[38;5;208m1. Create a token\x1b[0m\n  Create a namespaced token as described in Connect a log\n  source (man namespaces).  There's nothing special about Heroku\n  that requires its own token, but it's probably tidier if it's got its own.\n\n  \x1b[38;5;208m2. Add the drain\x1b[0m\n  For each Heroku app you want to ingest, run heroku drains:add with the\n  token embedded in the weft drain URL. Your token goes in the URL like so:\n\n    heroku drains:add \"https://api.w3ft.net/api/ingest/heroku/wft_a1b2c3d4e5f6...\" -a myapp\n    heroku drains:add \"https://api.w3ft.net/api/ingest/heroku/wft_a1b2c3d4e5f6...\" -a sister-app\n\n  Heroku starts delivering within a few seconds. Then confirm rows are landing\n  and, if you want nice, speedy lookups, approve a\n  schema (man overview). Heroku schemas are per\n  service, so it covers the web, cron and build files under it.\n\n  \x1b[38;5;208mHow dynos and procs map to log files\x1b[0m\n  The Heroku app name is the weft service, and the process type from the\n  procid (web, worker, router) is the log kind.\n  (See Namespaces for how every producer chooses its service name.)\n  The full procid (web.1, web.2) is stamped on instance, so cross-dyno\n  aggregation stays cheap and a single tail /log/.../web.log covers every web\n  dyno at once.\n\n  Weft maps Heroku sources to log files as follows:\n\n  \x1b[38;5;8mHeroku source              weft file                  metavariables\x1b[0m\n  \x1b[38;5;8m-------------------------  -------------------------  ------------------------\x1b[0m\n  myapp web dyno web.1       /log/<ns>/myapp/web.log    instance=web.1\n  myapp worker dyno          /log/<ns>/myapp/worker.log  instance=worker.1\n  worker.1\n  myapp router               /log/<ns>/myapp/router.log  instance=router\n  myapp heroku-postgres      /log/<ns>/myapp/heroku-postgres.log  instance=heroku-postgres\n  Platform events (deploys,  /log/<ns>/myapp/platform.log\n  restarts, scales)\n\n  (The presence of the <env> segment depends on how you created your token.\n  See Paths (man introduction) for the layout rules.)\n\n  \x1b[38;5;208mMetric lines auto-route to metrics.log\x1b[0m\n  Heroku's runtime-heroku-metrics labs feature (and most add-ons) emit\n  l2met-shaped lines like:\n\n    source=web.1 sample#memory_total=512.00MB sample#memory_rss=487.21MB sample#load_avg_1m=0.42\n\n  Weft detects these and routes them to <appname>/metrics.log, with the\n  key/value pairs converted to JSON (numeric where the value is\n  numeric, with trailing units like MB kept on a sibling\n  <key>_unit field). The original line also flows into the dyno's\n  log file, so grep memory_total /log/.../web.log matches it.\n\n    # Enable the metric stream on a Heroku app (one-time)\n    heroku labs:enable \"runtime-heroku-metrics\" -a myapp\n\n    # Then read it like any other JSON log source\n    weft sh\n    $ tail /log/myco/prod/myapp/metrics.log | jq 'select(.memory_total > 400)'\n"

// ManualMetavars — Meta-variables (generated from docs/manual/metavars.md).
const ManualMetavars = "METAVARS(1)                     Weft Commands                      METAVARS(1)\n\n  Each stored row carries a fixed set of indexed columns beside its text and JSON\n  payload. Meta-variables expose them in awk and jq. Filtering on one is the\n  fast path: weft skips data that can't match instead of parsing every row.\n\n  \x1b[38;5;8mawk                  jq                   Column          Source\x1b[0m\n  \x1b[38;5;8m-------------------  -------------------  --------------  --------------------\x1b[0m\n  NAMESPACE            $namespace           namespace       the <namespace> path\n                                                            segment / agent\n                                                            token's namespace\n  SERVICE              $service             service         source declaration,\n                                                            log filename, or\n                                                            HTTP ingest payload\n  KIND                 $kind                kind            the stream kind\n                                                            (main, heartbeats,\n                                                            metrics, runtime)\n  HOST (alias          $host (alias         instance        the agent's hostname\n  INSTANCE)            $instance)\n  LEVEL                $level               level           schema-extracted\n                                                            from the row\n  TS                   $ts                  ts              schema-extracted\n                                                            from the row\n  DEPLOYMENT           $deployment          deployment      weftd deployment:\n                                                            config (or k8s/ASG)\n  ENV                  $env                 env             bound to the agent\n                                                            token at mint time\n  VERSION              $version             version         weftd\n                                                            metavariables.version:\n  REGION               $region              region          cloud metadata probe\n                                                            at agent startup\n  CLUSTER              $cluster             cluster         weftd k8s mode\n  K8S_NAMESPACE        $k8s_namespace       k8s_namespace   weftd k8s mode\n  POD                  $pod                 pod             weftd k8s mode\n  CONTAINER            $container           container       weftd k8s mode\n  NODE                 $node                node            weftd\n                                                            metavariables.node:\n                                                            (never auto-derived)\n  TRACE_ID             $trace_id            trace_id        the SDK's trace ID,\n                                                            or schema-extracted\n                                                            from the row\n  SPAN_NAME            $span_name           span_name       on a span's lines,\n                                                            from the SDK header\n                                                            or the schema\n  SPAN_ID              $span_id             span_id         on a span's lines,\n                                                            from the SDK header\n                                                            or the schema\n  PARENT_SPAN_ID       $parent_span_id      parent_span_id  on a span's lines,\n                                                            the span that\n                                                            started it\n  DURATION_NS          $duration_ns         duration_ns     on the line that\n                                                            ends a span, its\n                                                            duration in\n                                                            nanoseconds\n  EVENT_ID             $event_id            event_id        shared by the lines\n                                                            of one multi-line\n                                                            event\n  LINE_NO              $line_no             line_no         the line's 0-based\n                                                            position within its\n                                                            event\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mSemantics\x1b[0m\n  - A row without a dimension carries an empty string: ENV == \"prod\" does not\n    match it, ENV != \"prod\" does. In jq $line_no and $duration_ns are\n    numbers ($duration_ns is null on a row without one); the rest are strings.\n  - EVENT_ID / LINE_NO tie together the lines of a multi-line event (a stack\n    trace): each line is its own row, sharing an EVENT_ID. A one-line event is\n    line 0 with an empty EVENT_ID, so LINE_NO == 0 selects the first line of\n    every event and EVENT_ID != \"\" only lines of multi-line events.\n  - NAMESPACE is weft's token grouping (the /log/<namespace>/... segment).\n    The Kubernetes namespace is K8S_NAMESPACE.\n  - REGION, DEPLOYMENT, HOST, CLUSTER and POD are query-time\n    dimensions, not path segments. For which dimensions appear in the path, see\n    Paths (man introduction).\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mNaming\x1b[0m\n  awk names are bare uppercase globals (ENV, POD) — never $ENV, which in\n  awk means \"field number ENV\". jq names are lowercase variables ($env,\n  $pod). $pod is always the indexed column and .pod always a key in the\n  row's JSON payload, even when a producer writes Pod or POD. A jq program\n  that rebinds a name (. as $env | ...) reads its own value.\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mSpeed\x1b[0m\n  Meta-variable filters are fast: awk 'ENV == \"prod\"', awk 'POD ~ \"api-7f9b\"',\n  jq 'select($level == \"ERROR\")'. Anything else reads and parses every row in\n  the time range: jq 'select(.feature_flag == \"abc\")', awk's numbered fields\n  ($3 == \"POST\"). A pipeline can mix both; the meta-variable filters narrow the\n  data first and the rest runs on what remains. For a dimension you query\n  constantly, ask for it to be indexed rather than filtering on the payload.\n"

// ManualNamespaces — Namespaces & tokens (generated from docs/manual/namespaces.md).
const ManualNamespaces = "NAMESPACES(1)                   Weft Commands                    NAMESPACES(1)\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mNamespaces\x1b[0m\n  Namespaces group related log sources. We usually think if namespaces as buckets\n  for a single application, but it's your bucket, so do what you want. Namespaces\n  are as granular as permission grants get, so keep that in mind when laying out\n  your log sources.\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mCreating a namespace\x1b[0m\n  To create a new namespace in your browser, go to Namespaces in the sidebar\n  and click new. Choose something easy to type for the name — it'll be in\n  the path of every log file you configure under it.\n\n  Or, from the CLI:\n\n    weft namespace create babbage\n\n  To remove one, weft namespace remove babbage. It refuses while the namespace\n  still has sources or live tokens, and lists them; --force removes the\n  namespace and everything in it. Logs already stored age out under normal\n  retention.\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mTokens\x1b[0m\n  Weft uses a simple token-based authentication for all log producers, whether\n  that be weftd, Render, Heroku, or Kinesis.  Each token is scoped to exactly one\n  namespace, but you can have as many as you like within a namespace. In fact, we\n  encourage it! You can optionally tag your tokens with an environment name,\n  allowing you to keep a staging, sandbox, and prod environment under the same\n  namespace but keep their logs separate.\n\n  You can always view your amassed sea of tokens under each namespace in the\n  browser, or in your terminal with weft token list.\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mCreating a token\x1b[0m\n  To create a token in the browser, go to Namespaces, select the namespace\n  you want the token to be in, and click new token. You can optionally bind a\n  environment tag (prod, staging, qa, etc.) and a label. What's the label\n  for? You tell me! It's nice to have options. The environment will have log file\n  path significance, whereas the label does not (but you can still filter on it).\n\n  From the CLI:\n\n    weft token create babbage --label api-prod --env prod\n\n  The token is only shown once regardless of where you create it, so put it\n  somewhere safe! If you lose it, revoke it and create a new one -- either by\n  clicking \"revoke\" in the browser or with weft token revoke --id <id> on the\n  command line.\n"

// ManualNotify — Notifications (generated from docs/manual/notify.md).
const ManualNotify = "NOTIFY(1)                       Weft Commands                        NOTIFY(1)\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mDefine a notification alias\x1b[0m\n  When an alert fires in a cron, --notify=<alias> dispatches to a\n  destination defined in /etc/notify.yaml. The alias is a name;\n  it fans out to one or more targets across four service types — Slack,\n  PagerDuty, webhook, and email.\n\n    # /etc/notify.yaml\n    ops:\n      slack:\n        - \"#alerts\"\n      pagerduty:\n        - ops-daytime\n      email:\n        - \"oncall@example.com\"\n\n  Edit it with vi /etc/notify.yaml. See help alerts for the full\n  grammar (severities, webhook headers, per-target mappings).\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mConnecting Slack and PagerDuty\x1b[0m\n  notify.yaml names destinations — the credentials behind them\n  live with your account. Connect them from the terminal with weft\n  integration or under Account → Integrations in the browser;\n  both do the same thing.\n\n    # PagerDuty — paste an integration (routing) key, give it a name.\n    # The name is what notify.yaml references under `pagerduty:`.\n    weft integration pagerduty add ops-daytime --key YOUR_ROUTING_KEY\n    weft integration pagerduty rm  ops-daytime\n\n    # Slack — authorize a workspace. Slack uses OAuth, so this opens a\n    # browser tab to grant access (like `weft login`); the CLI waits and\n    # confirms once the workspace is connected.\n    weft integration slack connect\n    weft integration slack rm\n\n    # See what's connected, plus the PagerDuty names notify.yaml can use.\n    weft integration list\n\n  A PagerDuty alias in notify.yaml must match a name you've registered\n  with weft integration pagerduty add. Slack channels are named\n  directly in notify.yaml (\"#alerts\") once the workspace is\n  connected.\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mWebhook and email need no account connection\x1b[0m\n  Webhook URLs and email addresses carry no account-level credential.\n  They are written into notify.yaml per alias:\n\n  Webhook header values are the exception: an Authorization token\n  or a signing secret is a credential, so cat /etc/notify.yaml (and\n  git log -p /etc/notify.yaml) shows each one as \"<redacted>\". To edit,\n  leave \"<redacted>\" where a value should stay unchanged, or write a real\n  value to replace it. A \"<redacted>\" on a webhook, header or alias that\n  has nothing stored is refused.\n\n    pages:\n      webhook:\n        - \"https://hooks.example.com/weft\"\n      email:\n        - \"oncall@example.com\"\n\n  Email delivery requires SMTP to be configured for your deployment.\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mTesting an alias\x1b[0m\n    echo \"test fire\" | alert --notify=ops\n\n  This sends to every target in the ops alias.\n\n  Interactive runs dispatch immediately and open no incident\n  (incidents belong to crons).\n"

// ManualIntroduction — Introduction (generated from docs/manual/query-logs.md).
const ManualIntroduction = "INTRODUCTION(1)                 Weft Commands                  INTRODUCTION(1)\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mThe weft shell\x1b[0m\n  The goal of weft is, in a nutshell, to enable efficient querying of distributed\n  log storage using the time-tested tools of Unix. As such, we've made an effort\n  to provide dopplegangers of enough of coreutils that you can mostly forget you\n  aren't just ssh'ed into your servers directly. Stitch together pipelines to\n  your heart's content. See Builtins (help builtins) for a complete list of\n  supported Unix commands and interpreters.\n\n  Some 1-liners are easier for our tools to translate into log queries than\n  others, and we've invested the most effort into AWK, sed, grep, and jq. Leaning\n  on these will result in the most efficient retrieval of rows from the log\n  store. We're working on exposing something like optimization hints in a coming\n  release.\n\n  Again, the principle of least surprise is our north star here. Hopefully you\n  never know you aren't on an old-school log server. And hopefully when you run\n  into that surprise, we've got nice clear error messages. But like us, weft is a\n  work in progress.\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mKey differences\x1b[0m\n  \x1b[38;5;208mSandboxness\x1b[0m\n  Weft is a log shell, and although it allows a certain amount of scripting for those purposes, it is not an operating system, a general purpose programming environment, or a VPS you control. There may be things you would like to do that you cannot. The veneer of Unix is only as thick as we needed it to be.\n\n  \x1b[38;5;208mRange\x1b[0m\n  The timerange of logs you are working with is displayed in your PS1. How far back in time you can set it depends on your plan. Spoiler, if you ship a lot of logs and you wanna query 3 months, it's gonna be slower. You can change it with the range command.\n\n  \x1b[38;5;208mMetavariables\x1b[0m\n  We inject some preset variables into invocations of AWK and jq that are helpful in narrowing down the lines you need; these are all indexed nicely so including them in an AWK pattern or a jq select() should get you your log lines faster. See the full reference for more details.\n\n  \x1b[38;5;208m\x1b[0m\n\x1b[38;5;208mSome Examples\x1b[0m\n    # Tail logs in real time\n    tail -f myapp.log\n\n    # Errors in the default range (narrow it with `range 1h..`)\n    cat myapp.log | awk 'LEVEL == \"ERROR\"'\n\n    # Requests by status code\n    cat nginx.log | jq '.status' | sort | uniq -c | sort -rn\n\n    # Everything in a namespace mentioning a failure\n    cat myco.log | grep \"connection refused\"\n\n    # Most recent 100 lines (cheap)\n    tail -n 100 myapp.log\n\n    # Production errors for one deployment, JSON form\n    cat /log/myco/prod/api/main.log \\\n      | jq 'select($level == \"ERROR\" and $deployment == \"api-east\")'\n\n    # Keep a result set and slice it several ways\n    cat api.log | awk 'LEVEL == \"ERROR\"' > /tmp/errs\n    grep timeout /tmp/errs | sort | uniq -c | sort -rn\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mPaths\x1b[0m\n    /log/<namespace>/<service>/<kind>.log          3 segments: all envs\n    /log/<namespace>/<env>/<service>/<kind>.log    4 segments: one env\n\n  <kind> is the stream: main for ordinary logs, plus heartbeats,\n  metrics and runtime for services that emit telemetry. Which form a\n  service has depends on whether its producer token was minted with --env;\n  ls /log/myco shows env directories next to env-less services.\n\n  - cat /log/myco/prod/api/main.log reads one env.\n  - cat /log/myco/api/main.log reads that service across every env.\n  - cat myco.log reads the whole namespace; filter with ENV == \"prod\".\n  - ls /log/myco/api lists a service's kinds.\n  - Globs only pick kinds inside one service: /log/myco/prod/api/*.log.\n    Globs that cross services or namespaces (cat *.log,\n    /log/myco/*/api/main.log) are refused.\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mRetention\x1b[0m\n  Your plan sets how far back queries can read (see the Account page); older\n  data is filtered out of every query. Archived data is queried transparently —\n  range 180d.. reads recent and archived data in one pass, with a little extra\n  latency on the cold side.\n\n  See Builtins (help builtins) for the command list,\n  Meta-variables (man metavars) for the indexed columns, and\n  Scratch space and cookbook (man tmp) for /tmp and worked recipes.\n"

// ManualConnectRender — Connect a Render service (generated from docs/manual/render.md).
const ManualConnectRender = "CONNECT-RENDER(1)               Weft Commands                CONNECT-RENDER(1)\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mConnect a Render service\x1b[0m\n  Weft accepts logs directly from Render's built-in Log Streams feature using\n  syslog over TLS. This page covers the Render-specific parts; for an overview, see Connect a log source (man overview).\n\n  \x1b[38;5;208m1. Create a token\x1b[0m\n  Create a namespaced token as described in Connect a log\n  source (man namespaces). There's nothing special about Render\n  that requires its own token, but it's probably tidier if it's got its own.\n\n  \x1b[38;5;208m2. Add the log stream in Render\x1b[0m\n  In the Render dashboard, click Integrations → Observability in the sidebar.\n  Scroll down to Log Streams, add a stream, and configure a generic/custom\n  syslog destination. Paste in the token from Step 1, along with the endpoint\n  (render.syslog.w3ft.net:6514).\n\n  Render starts streaming within a few seconds. If you want nice, speedy lookups,\n  approve a schema (man overview). Render\n  schemas are per service, so it covers the web, cron and build files under it.\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mFinding your logs\x1b[0m\n  Weft maps Render services to log files as follows:\n\n  \x1b[38;5;8mRender source                     weft file                     metavariables\x1b[0m\n  \x1b[38;5;8m--------------------------------  ----------------------------  --------------\x1b[0m\n  myservice web instance web-9nk6q  /log/<ns>/myservice/web.log   instance=9nk6q\n  myservice cron job cron-stqq5     /log/<ns>/myservice/cron.log  instance=stqq5\n  myservice build output bld-4fk2b  /log/<ns>/myservice/bld.log   instance=4fk2b\n\n  (The presence of the <env> segment depends on how you created your token.\n  See Paths (man introduction) for the layout rules.)\n"

// ManualSchemaSetup — Check schema setup (generated from docs/manual/schema-setup.md).
const ManualSchemaSetup = "SCHEMA-SETUP(1)                 Weft Commands                  SCHEMA-SETUP(1)\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mCheck schema setup\x1b[0m\n  Once logs are arriving, weft auto-detects the schema from the first\n  batch of lines. With weftd, logs wait for you to approve it; with\n  push sources (Heroku, Render, CloudWatch) logs flow raw while the sample\n  fills and gain parsed fields once you approve. Go to Namespaces to see detected apps and\n  their field schemas.\n\n  If you see a pending source notification on the dashboard, weft\n  has received data and needs you to confirm the detected\n  schema. Review the sample lines and approve.\n\n  Two fields come from parsing each log line per the source's schema:\n\n  - TS — timestamp\n  - LEVEL — log level (ERROR, WARN, INFO, DEBUG)\n\n  Every other dimension (SERVICE, HOST, ENV, REGION, ...) is stamped by\n  weftd, the agent token, or cloud metadata rather than parsed from the line;\n  Meta-variables (man metavars) has the full table and how each is sourced.\n"

// ManualScripts — Custom scripts (generated from docs/manual/scripts.md).
const ManualScripts = "SCRIPTS(1)                      Weft Commands                       SCRIPTS(1)\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mCustom scripts in /bin\x1b[0m\n  Anything you can type at the shell prompt can be saved as a named script under\n  /bin/. Scripts are available from every shell session and every cron\n  belonging to your account. Create scripts with vi or with cat - > myscript.\n  Writing the file validates the script for syntax errors and some\n  weft-specific semantics.\n\n  \x1b[38;5;208mWriting a script\x1b[0m\n  The script body is a pipeline. Positional parameters ($1, $2,\n  …) bind to the arguments the caller passes at invocation.\n\n    # /bin/count_errors\n    cat /log/$1 | grep ERROR | wc -l\n\n  Invoke it like any other command:\n\n    count_errors myco/prod/api/main.log      # number of ERROR lines in that source\n    count_errors myco/prod/api/main.log | sort -n   # scripts compose into pipelines\n\n  A script, bash or Lua, can also sit at a later stage of a pipeline,\n  after a log read: tail -n 500 /log/myco/prod/api/main.log | myscript.\n\n  Small, pipelined scripts are optimized well by weft. Larger scripts are supported.\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mLanguage support\x1b[0m\n  The weft shell supports a subset of bash and Lua.\n  Scripts default to bash; declare Lua with a shebang on the first\n  line.\n\n  \x1b[38;5;208mBash\x1b[0m\n  Effectively the full bash grammar is available:\n\n  - pipes (|), redirects (>, >>, <, 2>), subshells (( ... ))\n    and command groups ({ ...; })\n  - variable and parameter expansion ($var, ${var}, $1, $@,\n    ${var:-default}), command substitution ($(...) and backticks),\n    arithmetic expansion ($(( ... )))\n  - conditionals (if/elif/else/fi), case/esac, and loops\n    (for, while, until)\n  - user-defined functions (name() { ...; })\n  - the standard text toolkit as builtins: grep, awk, sed, jq,\n    cut, sort, uniq, wc, tee, tr, xargs, head, tail,\n    cat, echo, less\n\n  \x1b[38;5;208mLua\x1b[0m\n  Lua suits arithmetic, string manipulation and JSON assembly.\n\n    #!/usr/bin/env lua\n    -- /bin/report\n    local name = arg[1]\n    local avg = weft.avg(name, \"1h\")\n    print(string.format(\"%s averaged %.2f over the last hour\", name, avg))\n\n  Weft provides Lua 5.1, including the standard table, string,\n  math, and coroutine libraries, plus a restricted os (os.clock,\n  os.date, os.time, os.difftime). dofile, loadfile, load,\n  loadstring, and require are disabled, and scripts time out after 30\n  seconds.\n\n  On top of the standard library, weft adds:\n\n  \x1b[38;5;8mGlobal                           Purpose\x1b[0m\n  \x1b[38;5;8m-------------------------------  ---------------------------------------------\x1b[0m\n  arg                              Positional arguments — arg[1], arg[2], …\n  print(...)                       Write to stdout (tab-separated args, newline)\n  eprint(...)                      Same shape, writes to stderr\n  io.write(...)                    Stdout without trailing newline\n  io.read(\"*l\" | \"*a\" | \"*n\")      Read a line, all of stdin, or a number\n  lines()                          Iterator over stdin lines — for line in\n                                   lines() do ... end\n  json.encode(v) / json.decode(s)  JSON ↔ Lua table\n  weft.*                           Cron aggregates and predicates — see Weft\n                                   functions\n\n  Because Lua can read stdin with lines() or io.read(\"*l\"), a Lua\n  script can act as a pipeline stage:\n\n    #!/usr/bin/env lua\n    -- /bin/extract_ids (for a source whose lines are JSON objects)\n    for line in lines() do\n      local obj = json.decode(line)\n      if obj.user_id then print(obj.user_id) end\n    end\n\n    cat /log/myco/prod/api/events.log | extract_ids | sort -u | wc -l\n\n  Weft optimizes coreutils pipelines more than Lua scripts. For best\n  performance, put Lua at the end of a pipeline.\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mEditing a script with vi\x1b[0m\n  vi /bin/<name> opens the script for editing. After :w, the\n  shell drops you into a follow-up buffer asking for an optional\n  commit message — :wq to record, :q! to skip. See git for\n  the full version-control workflow, including git log -- /bin/<name>,\n  git show, git diff, git restore, and git commit --amend.\n\n  The five most recent past versions are kept; older saves are\n  discarded. Versions past the cap cannot be recovered.\n"

// ManualSharing — Sharing scripts (generated from docs/manual/sharing.md).
const ManualSharing = "SHARING(1)                      Weft Commands                       SHARING(1)\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mSharing a script with a teammate\x1b[0m\n  When a /bin/ script is worth showing to someone else — a runbook\n  link, a teammate investigating the same incident — clipboard builds\n  a deep link that reproduces what you're looking at and copies it to\n  your system clipboard.\n\n    clipboard /bin/error-rate\n    copied: https://w3ft.net/s/k3Fz9aQ2xB\n\n  The script must already exist in /bin/, and the link's host depends on\n  your deployment.\n\n  Paste the URL anywhere — Slack, an incident timeline, a runbook —\n  and a signed-in teammate who clicks it lands in the web shell with\n  the script invocation pre-filled. They review, hit Enter, and see\n  the same output you saw.\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mWhat the link encodes\x1b[0m\n  The URL is a short name. Weft stores three things behind it,\n  and sharing the same script, range and args again gives the same link:\n\n  - the script path, always /bin/<name>.\n  - the time window. Defaults to your shell's current range (the\n    one range shows). Override with --range=<spec>.\n  - the args, anything you typed after -- at the clipboard\n    invocation. Put --range before --; anything after -- is passed to\n    the script as-is. Args are joined with spaces; original shell quoting is\n    not preserved.\n\n  A link always opens the script's current version. If a teammate needs to\n  see how the script behaved before someone edited it, they pair the\n  link with git log -- /bin/<name> to read the history.\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mWhy share a script link\x1b[0m\n  A /bin/ script with a deep link keeps the time window, keeps quoted\n  awk and jq programs intact, and handles parameterised scripts. The script\n  body lives in your org's /bin/, version-tracked by git, and the link\n  carries the runtime context (range + args).\n\n  To share a pipeline that is not yet a /bin/ script, save it first:\n\n    cat - > /bin/check-foo\n    # paste the pipeline, then ctrl-d\n    clipboard /bin/check-foo\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mExamples\x1b[0m\n    clipboard /bin/error-rate\n                            current range, no args\n\n    clipboard /bin/error-rate -- --app=loom --since=1h\n                            pre-fills error-rate --app=loom --since=1h\n\n    clipboard /bin/scan --range=24h..\n                            pins a 24-hour window regardless of your range\n\n    clipboard /bin/check --range=yesterday..today -- prod\n                            yesterday's window, one positional arg\n\n  See man clipboard for the full command reference.\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mAccess\x1b[0m\n  The link grants no access. It opens for signed-in members of\n  your org; anyone else gets \"that link doesn't exist\". Recipients also\n  need the namespace permissions required to run the script. A link pasted\n  into a public channel leaks no log data. It carries only a script invocation\n  an authorized teammate could already construct.\n"

// ManualTeams — Teams & permissions (generated from docs/manual/team.md).
const ManualTeams = "TEAMS(1)                        Weft Commands                         TEAMS(1)\n\n\x1b[38;5;208m/etc/passwd\x1b[0m\n  Your org's members and what they can do is reflected on the accounts page, as\n  well as in the weft shell in /etc/passwd:\n\n    $ cat /etc/passwd\n    USER             ROLE     STATUS    GROUPS\n    alice@acme.com   admin    active    binmgr  cronmgr  configmgr  nsmgr  payments  payments+tokens\n    bob@acme.com     member   active    binmgr  cronmgr  payments  payments+tokens\n    carol@acme.com   member   invited   —\n\n  Users are added or removed in the shell adduser|deluser <email address>, or\n  in the browser, account -> team. In either case, an invite will be\n  emailed. You must have the admin role to add or remove users.\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mGroups\x1b[0m\n  There are two main types of group:\n\n  Manager groups gate org-wide directories and namespace management:\n\n  \x1b[38;5;8mGroup      Grants\x1b[0m\n  \x1b[38;5;8m---------  -------------------------------------------\x1b[0m\n  binmgr     edit /bin (functions)\n  cronmgr    edit /cron.d (crons)\n  configmgr  edit /etc (notify.yaml, dashboards/\\*.yaml)\n  nsmgr      create and manage namespaces\n\n  Namespace groups control log access and token creation:\n\n  \x1b[38;5;8mGroup               Grants\x1b[0m\n  \x1b[38;5;8m------------------  -----------------------------------------\x1b[0m\n  <namespace>         read /log/<namespace>\n  <namespace>+tokens  create producer tokens for that namespace\n\n  A member can have either or both.\n\n  Admins hold groups as other members do, and the roster lists the groups they have.\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mGranting and revoking\x1b[0m\n  Once someone is a member, add or drop their groups:\n\n    adduser dana@acme.com payments         # read payments logs\n    adduser dana@acme.com payments+tokens  # ...and mint producer tokens\n    adduser dana@acme.com cronmgr          # ...and edit /cron.d\n    deluser dana@acme.com payments         # revoke read\n\n  There is no clicky version of this -- you must use the shell. Same for getting the deets on what groups a user belongs to:\n\n    groups dana@acme.com                   # what dana can do\n    id dana@acme.com                       # role + groups\n\n  (groups and id with no argument describe you.)\n"

// ManualTmp — Scratch space and cookbook (generated from docs/manual/tmp.md).
const ManualTmp = "TMP(1)                          Weft Commands                           TMP(1)\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mScratch space (/tmp)\x1b[0m\n  Every weft account has a scratch area at /tmp. Use it to save the output\n  of a pipeline, take notes during an incident, or hold a filtered result\n  set long enough to slice it a few different ways. Write with >, append\n  with >>, edit with vi, list with ls, remove with rm.\n\n    # Save a filtered result set\n    cat /log/myco/api/main.log | awk 'LEVEL == \"ERROR\"' > /tmp/errs\n\n    # Take a look\n    wc -l /tmp/errs\n    grep timeout /tmp/errs | sort | uniq -c | sort -rn | head\n\n    # Keep adding to it later (5xx responses among the last 200 rows)\n    tail -n 200 /log/myco/api/main.log | awk '$6 ~ /status=5/' >> /tmp/errs\n\n    # Organise an incident\n    mkdir /tmp/incident-2026-04-22\n    cat /log/myco/api/main.log | grep 'req_id=abc123' > /tmp/incident-2026-04-22/trace\n    vi /tmp/incident-2026-04-22/notes\n    ls -l /tmp/incident-2026-04-22\n\n    # Compare two result sets\n    cp /tmp/errs /tmp/errs.bak\n    tail -n 1000 /log/myco/api/main.log | awk 'LEVEL == \"ERROR\"' > /tmp/errs   # errors among the last 1000 rows\n    diff /tmp/errs.bak /tmp/errs           # what's new in the most recent slice\n\n    # comm and join need sorted input\n    sort /tmp/errs.bak > /tmp/errs.bak.sorted\n    sort /tmp/errs > /tmp/errs.sorted\n    comm /tmp/errs.bak.sorted /tmp/errs.sorted   # three-column shared/unique view\n\n    # Relate two saved files by their first column\n    printf 'api 3\\nweb 1\\n' > /tmp/errs-by-service\n    printf 'api oncall-a\\nweb oncall-b\\n' > /tmp/owners\n    join /tmp/errs-by-service /tmp/owners        # api 3 oncall-a, web 1 oncall-b\n\n    # Rename, keep alive\n    mv /tmp/errs /tmp/incident-errs\n    touch /tmp/incident-errs               # reset the 48h clock\n\n    # Clean up when you're done\n    rm -rf /tmp/incident-2026-04-22\n\n  \x1b[38;5;208mLifetime and limits\x1b[0m\n  - 48 hours. /tmp is durable across logouts and shared by every member of\n    your account (no other account sees it), but a file is deleted 48 hours after\n    its last write. >, >>, cp, mv and touch reset the clock; ls -l\n    shows the mtime. For long-term results, save the pipeline as a cron instead\n    (see Crons); cron results keep your plan's full history.\n  - Size. A per-file cap and a total allowance, both set by your plan (see\n    the Account page). A write that would exceed either fails and writes\n    nothing. ls -lh /tmp shows sizes.\n  - Not scripts. Files in /tmp are never executed; scripts live in /bin.\n  - No follow. tail -f ... > /tmp/x is refused; drop -f.\n  - Crossing out. cp and mv work only inside /tmp. Use\n    cat /tmp/x > /bin/y to move a file out and cat /bin/y > /tmp/x to edit\n    a copy.\n\n  The Unix tools for comparing saved files (diff, comm, join, paste,\n  wc) are listed in Builtins (help builtins).\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mCookbook\x1b[0m\n  \x1b[38;5;208mTriage a spike\x1b[0m\n    cat /log/myco/api/main.log | awk 'LEVEL == \"ERROR\"' > /tmp/errs\n    awk '{ print SERVICE, HOST }' /tmp/errs | sort | uniq -c | sort -rn | head\n\n  \x1b[38;5;208mLevel histogram by region for one deployment\x1b[0m\n    cat /log/myco/prod/api/main.log \\\n      | awk 'DEPLOYMENT == \"api\" {print REGION, LEVEL}' \\\n      | sort | uniq -c\n\n  \x1b[38;5;208mProd errors for one deployment, across the namespace\x1b[0m\n    cat myco.log \\\n      | awk 'ENV == \"prod\" && LEVEL == \"ERROR\" && DEPLOYMENT == \"api\"' \\\n      | head -50\n\n  ENV and DEPLOYMENT narrow the read before any row is parsed. The jq form\n  is jq 'select($env == \"prod\" and $deployment == \"api\")'.\n\n  \x1b[38;5;208mWhole stack traces for matching errors\x1b[0m\n    cat /log/myco/api/main.log | awk 'LINE_NO == 0 && /timeout/' > /tmp/first-lines\n\n  Lines of one multi-line event share EVENT_ID; LINE_NO == 0 is the first.\n\n  \x1b[38;5;208mWhat's new since an hour ago\x1b[0m\n    cp /tmp/errs /tmp/errs.bak\n    tail -n 1000 /log/myco/api/main.log | awk 'LEVEL == \"ERROR\"' > /tmp/errs\n    diff /tmp/errs.bak /tmp/errs\n\n  For comm, sort first: sort /tmp/a > /tmp/a.s.\n\n  \x1b[38;5;208mRelate two saved files\x1b[0m\n    printf 'api 3\\nweb 1\\n' > /tmp/errs-by-service\n    printf 'api oncall-a\\nweb oncall-b\\n' > /tmp/owners\n    join /tmp/errs-by-service /tmp/owners     # api 3 oncall-a / web 1 oncall-b\n\n  \x1b[38;5;208mOrganise an incident\x1b[0m\n    mkdir /tmp/incident-2026-04-22\n    cat /log/myco/api/main.log | grep 'req_id=abc123' > /tmp/incident-2026-04-22/trace\n    vi /tmp/incident-2026-04-22/notes\n    touch /tmp/incident-2026-04-22/trace      # keep it past 48h\n    rm -rf /tmp/incident-2026-04-22           # when done\n"

// ManualTrace — Tracing a request (generated from docs/manual/trace.md).
const ManualTrace = "TRACE(1)                        Weft Commands                         TRACE(1)\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mTracing a request\x1b[0m\n  trace follows one request through every service it touched. Give it a trace\n  ID and it returns every log line carrying that ID, from every source you can\n  read, in time order. Add --spans for a per-operation breakdown of where the\n  request's time went, or ask trace spans how an operation performs across all\n  requests.\n\n    trace 4bf92f3577b34da6a3ce929d0e0e4736\n    trace 4bf92f3577b34da6a3ce929d0e0e4736 --spans\n    trace spans\n    trace spans db.query --by service\n\n  Like cat, trace reads the current range (man range), so a trace older than\n  the window needs a wider one first.\n\n  \x1b[38;5;208mGetting trace IDs into your logs\x1b[0m\n  A line has a trace ID when the weft SDK wrote it, with the ID your request\n  already carries. The SDKs set up the rest:\n\n  - Join a trace. The Go and Python SDKs adopt the traceparent header of an\n    incoming HTTP request, or start a new trace when there is none. Go:\n    weft.TraceMiddleware. Python: weft.WSGITraceMiddleware and\n    weft.ASGITraceMiddleware.\n  - Carry it across calls. weft.TraceHeaders (Go) and weft.trace_headers\n    (Python) give the headers for an outgoing HTTP request. For gRPC, install the\n    weft.GRPC interceptors (Go) or weft.grpc (Python) on both ends.\n  - Time the work. weft.StartSpan (Go) and weft.start_span (Python) log a\n    span with a name, an ID, its parent and, when it ends, its duration. A span\n    started inside another becomes its child.\n  - Log through the SDK. Lines written through slog with the request's\n    context (Go), or the standard logging module (Python), carry the trace and\n    the current span automatically. See the SDK READMEs for the details of each.\n\n  Trace IDs and spans are columns on every row: TRACE_ID, SPAN_NAME,\n  SPAN_ID, PARENT_SPAN_ID and DURATION_NS (see meta-variables (man metavars)).\n\n  \x1b[38;5;208mtrace ID\x1b[0m\n    $ trace 4bf92f3577b34da6a3ce929d0e0e4736\n    # 7 line(s) across 3 service(s), 2026-10-19T14:02:11Z to 2026-10-19T14:02:12Z\n               [acme/api]      GET /orders/812\n    ██         [acme/orders]   loading order 812\n               [acme/orders]   db.query took 21ms\n\n  The first line counts the lines, the services they came from and the time they\n  span. Each row after it has a badge for the service that wrote it, in a color\n  that stays the same for that service, and a bar showing how long it was since the\n  previous line, so a stall shows up as a long bar.\n\n  Pipe it like any other source, and the badges and summary go away, leaving the\n  lines themselves. Every row carries $namespace and $service if you need to\n  tell the sources apart:\n\n    trace 4bf92f3577b34da6a3ce929d0e0e4736 | grep -i error\n    trace 4bf92f3577b34da6a3ce929d0e0e4736 | jq -r '$service + \": \" + .msg'\n\n  \x1b[38;5;208mtrace ID --spans\x1b[0m\n    $ trace 4bf92f3577b34da6a3ce929d0e0e4736 --spans\n    NAME          BAR         COUNT     TOTAL  ERRORS\n    db.query      ██████████      3      38ms       0\n    orders.load   ███             1      11ms       0\n    api.handle    ██              1       7ms       0\n\n  One row per span name in the trace. COUNT is how many times it ran. TOTAL is\n  self time: the span's duration minus the time its children covered, with\n  concurrent children counted once. It answers \"where did this request's time\n  actually go\", where a parent span's duration mostly reflects the work it handed\n  off. ERRORS counts the span's error lines.\n\n  Self time is only as complete as the trace: a child that logged nothing, or\n  that is older than the current range, leaves its time on its parent.\n\n  In the terminal, trace <id> --spans opens an interactive view:\n\n  \x1b[38;5;8mKey             Does\x1b[0m\n  \x1b[38;5;8m--------------  --------------------------------------------------------------\x1b[0m\n  ↑ ↓ or j k  move\n  g G             first, last\n  Enter           list the selected span's occurrences, with the trace each came\n                  from and its self time\n  Esc             back to the list from the occurrences; leave the view from the\n                  list\n  q               leave the view\n\n  Piped, it gives one JSON line per finished span or span error, ready for jq:\n\n    {\"name\":\"db.query\",\"namespace\":\"acme\",\"service\":\"orders\",\"trace_id\":\"4bf9…\",\"span_id\":\"a1\",\"parent_id\":\"b2\",\"dur_ns\":21000000,\"ts_ns\":1760882531000000000,\"level\":\"INFO\"}\n\n  ts_ns is when the span ended, so it started at ts_ns - dur_ns.\n\n  \x1b[38;5;208mtrace spans\x1b[0m\n    $ trace spans\n    NAME          BAR         COUNT     AVG     P50     P99     MIN     MAX  ERRORS\n    db.query      ██████████  18420   500µs   410µs   2.3ms    90µs   8.1ms       3\n    api.handle    ███          9102   1.2ms   1.1ms   4.8ms   410µs   9.9ms       0\n\n  Every span name in the current range, aggregated across all traces. Add a name\n  to look at one, or --by service to split each name by the service that ran\n  it, since the same name in two services is usually two different things:\n\n    trace spans db.query\n    trace spans --by service\n\n  The shards compute these in the database, so the cost follows the number of\n  span names, not the number of spans. COUNT, ERRORS, AVG, MIN and MAX\n  are exact. P50 and P99 are approximate: within 1% of the true value.\n\n  trace spans has no self time, since it sees spans one name at a time and can't\n  see their children; use trace <id> --spans for that.\n\n  The interactive view works like the one above. Enter on a name lists its\n  slowest occurrences, each with the trace ID to hand to trace.\n\n    trace spans db.query --samples\n\n  prints the same list: the 50 slowest finished occurrences of a name, one JSON\n  line each in the shape above.\n\n  \x1b[38;5;208mUsing the results\x1b[0m\n  Piped, trace spans gives one JSON line per name (or per name and service):\n\n    {\"name\":\"db.query\",\"count\":18420,\"errors\":3,\"total_ns\":9210000000,\"avg_ns\":500000,\n     \"p50_ns\":410000,\"p99_ns\":2300000,\"min_ns\":90000,\"max_ns\":8100000}\n\n  With --by service, namespace and service are added. So you can filter,\n  reshape, chart or alert on it like any other source:\n\n    trace spans | jq -r 'select(.p99_ns > 1000000000) | .name'\n    trace spans db.query | jq '.p99_ns / 1000000' | alert above 250 --notify=ops\n\n  The second line is a cron-ready check that pages when db.query's 99th percentile\n  passes 250ms.\n\n  \x1b[38;5;208mWhat counts as a span\x1b[0m\n  A finished span is a row with a DURATION_NS; it counts toward COUNT and the\n  timings. A span's start line has no duration and isn't counted. A row at level\n  ERROR that names a span is a span error and counts toward ERRORS. A source\n  with no span rows, such as one that logs plain text without the SDK or a schema\n  that extracts spans, has nothing for trace spans to find.\n"

// ManualWeftFuncs — Weft functions (generated from docs/manual/weft-funcs.md).
const ManualWeftFuncs = "WEFT-FUNCS(1)                   Weft Commands                    WEFT-FUNCS(1)\n\n  The weft::* family reads and reasons about your own cron history\n  from inside a pipeline. There are three\n  shapes:\n\n  - Value funcs return the most recent value, an aggregate over a\n    window, or an anchor like the slope between two endpoints.\n  - Predicates return a boolean — \"is this cron above 50?\",\n    \"did it just change?\" — for composing alert conditions across\n    multiple crons.\n  - Stdin comparators turn a piped value into a boolean.\n\n  The same set is available in two host languages:\n\n  \x1b[38;5;8mSurface       Syntax           Example\x1b[0m\n  \x1b[38;5;8m------------  ---------------  ----------------------------\x1b[0m\n  Bash builtin  weft::name args  weft::avg error-rate 1h\n  Lua function  weft.name(args)  weft.avg(\"error-rate\", \"1h\")\n\n  Bash uses :: as the namespace separator; Lua uses . for table\n  access. The arguments are the same in both.\n\n  For composing predicates into composite alerts, see\n  Alerting strategies.\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mValue functions\x1b[0m\n  Each takes a cron name, returns a value, and (with the exception of\n  weft::latest and weft::previous) a duration window. Durations are a\n  number and a unit: 30s, 5m, 1h, 6h, plus d for a day (24\n  hours) and w for a week (7 days). Units combine: 1d12h.\n\n  \x1b[38;5;8mBash                   Lua                     Returns  Notes\x1b[0m\n  \x1b[38;5;8m---------------------  ----------------------  -------  ----------------------\x1b[0m\n  weft::latest <cron>    weft.latest(cron)       string   Trimmed stdout of the\n                                                          most recent run\n  weft::previous <cron>  weft.previous(cron)     string   Stdout of the\n                                                          second-most-recent run\n                                                          (\"\" if fewer than 2\n                                                          exist)\n  weft::avg <cron>       weft.avg(cron, dur)     number   Mean of numeric\n  <dur>                                                   results in window\n  weft::min <cron>       weft.min(cron, dur)     number   Smallest numeric\n  <dur>                                                   result in window\n  weft::max <cron>       weft.max(cron, dur)     number   Largest numeric result\n  <dur>                                                   in window\n  weft::sum <cron>       weft.sum(cron, dur)     number   Sum of numeric results\n  <dur>                                                   in window\n  weft::stddev <cron>    weft.stddev(cron, dur)  number   Population stddev;\n  <dur>                                                   needs ≥2 results\n  weft::count <cron>     weft.count(cron, dur)   number   Count of results\n  <dur>                                                   (rows) in window,\n                                                          numeric or not\n  weft::percentile       weft.percentile(cron,   number   Linearly-interpolated\n  <cron> <dur> <p>       dur, p)                          p-th percentile, p ∈\n                                                          [0,100]\n  weft::first <cron>     weft.first(cron, dur)   number   Oldest numeric value\n  <dur>                                                   still in window\n  weft::delta <cron>     weft.delta(cron, dur)   number   latest − first over\n  <dur>                                                   window\n  weft::slope <cron>     weft.slope(cron, dur)   number   delta / window_seconds\n  <dur>\n  weft::rate <cron>      weft.rate(cron, dur)    number   sum / window_seconds\n  <dur>\n\n  \x1b[38;5;208mCaps\x1b[0m\n  The window caps follow compute cost:\n\n  - Aggregates (avg, min, max, sum, stddev, count,\n    percentile, rate) cap at 24h (1d). They touch every result in\n    the window.\n  - Endpoint funcs (first, delta, slope) cap at 14d. They\n    read only the oldest result in the window and the latest, so a\n    longer window is cheap.\n  - latest / previous are point reads — no window.\n\n  \x1b[38;5;208mNumeric parsing\x1b[0m\n  Aggregates parse each run's stdout as a float and skip\n  rows that don't parse. They error when nothing in the window is\n  numeric (and weft::stddev also errors when fewer than 2 numeric\n  samples exist).\n\n  weft::latest and weft::previous return whatever the cron wrote,\n  including non-numeric strings. Cast in Lua with tonumber(...).\n\n  \x1b[38;5;208mRetention\x1b[0m\n  The aggregates read whatever history your plan's retention covers;\n  older runs are not visible to the helpers.\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mPredicates\x1b[0m\n  Predicates return a boolean. In bash that's an exit code (0 = fire,\n  non-zero = no fire), letting you compose with native && || !.\n  In Lua the return is a plain bool, composable with and or not.\n\n  On absent data, non-numeric values, or cold-history conditions,\n  predicates return false: when the answer is unknown, the predicate\n  does not fire. The alert builtin behaves the same way on these\n  conditions. is_absent fires when there is no data.\n\n  \x1b[38;5;8mBash                      Lua                        Fires when\x1b[0m\n  \x1b[38;5;8m------------------------  -------------------------  -------------------------\x1b[0m\n  weft::is_above <cron>     weft.is_above(cron, thr,   latest > thr (or min over\n  <thr> [<dur>              [dur, [avg]])              dur > thr, or avg > thr\n  [--average]]                                         with --average)\n  weft::is_below <cron>     weft.is_below(cron, thr,   latest < thr (or max over\n  <thr> [<dur>              [dur, [avg]])              dur < thr, or avg < thr\n  [--average]]                                         with --average)\n  weft::is_absent <cron>    weft.is_absent(cron,       the cron has no result:\n  [<dur>]                   [dur])                     it doesn't exist, has\n                                                       never run, or its latest\n                                                       output is empty (with\n                                                       dur: no result landed in\n                                                       the last dur). A bad or\n                                                       over-cap dur (more than\n                                                       24h, 1d) or an unreadable\n                                                       history doesn't fire; the\n                                                       bash form says why on\n                                                       stderr\n  weft::has_changed <cron>  weft.has_changed(cron)     latest ≠ previous tick\n  weft::is_rising <cron>    weft.is_rising(cron,       (latest −\n  [<pct> [<dur>]]           [pct, [dur]])              baseline)/baseline ≥\n                                                       pct% (baseline = prev\n                                                       tick, or window avg if\n                                                       dur given)\n  weft::is_falling <cron>   weft.is_falling(cron,      (baseline −\n  [<pct> [<dur>]]           [pct, [dur]])              latest)/baseline ≥ pct%\n  weft::is_outlier <cron>   weft.is_outlier(cron, n,   \\|latest − avg\\| >\n  <n> <dur>                 dur, [dir])                n·stddev over dur\n  [--above|--below]\n  weft::is_seasonal <cron>  weft.is_seasonal(cron, n,  \\|latest −\n  <n> <period> <dur>        period, dur)               same-time-of-period avg\\|\n                                                       > n·stddev\n\n  \x1b[38;5;208mAnomaly guards\x1b[0m\n  is_outlier and is_seasonal both gate against zero-stddev histories:\n  if the historical stddev drops below 0.01 · |avg|, the predicate\n  returns false. is_seasonal\n  additionally requires at least 4 same-time samples.\n\n  \x1b[38;5;208mSeasonal alignment\x1b[0m\n  For is_seasonal, the period is the recurring interval and\n  window is how far back to sample. With period=1d window=4d, the\n  predicate compares the latest value to the four values captured at\n  this same hour on each of the last four days. With period=1w\n  window=8w, it compares to the same hour of the same weekday over\n  the last eight weeks. Period alignment uses UTC.\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mStdin comparators\x1b[0m\n  To compare an aggregate's stdout with a threshold, pipe it through\n  one of:\n\n  \x1b[38;5;8mBash               Fires when\x1b[0m\n  \x1b[38;5;8m-----------------  ---------------------------\x1b[0m\n  weft::is_gt <thr>  stdin parses as float > thr\n  weft::is_lt <thr>  stdin parses as float < thr\n\n    weft::avg error-rate 5m | weft::is_gt 50\n\n  In Lua, use the native > and < operators:\n  weft.avg(\"error-rate\", \"5m\") > 50.\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mSee also\x1b[0m\n  - Crons — how cron results get persisted and which weft::*\n    funcs read them back.\n  - Alerting strategies — patterns for composing predicates into\n    composite alerts, the multi-row tag-based form, debouncing, and\n    outlier vs seasonal guidance.\n  - Custom scripts — using weft.* from Lua /bin/ scripts.\n  - man alert — the alert builtin reference.\n"

// ManualWeftd — weftd (generated from docs/manual/weftd.md).
const ManualWeftd = "WEFTD(1)                        Weft Commands                         WEFTD(1)\n\n  weftd is weft's own log agent.  It tails files and systemd units on a Linux\n  host and streams structured rows to weft over gRPC. It is the fastest way you\n  can get your logs into weft, and since weft was designed so we could feel like\n  we were running tail -f right on the host, it's what we recommend you use if\n  you can.\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mConnect a host with weftd\x1b[0m\n  This page covers the weftd-specific parts; for an overview, see Connect a log\n  source (man overview).\n\n  \x1b[38;5;208m1. Install\x1b[0m\n  Download weftd from the Downloads page. Or just use curl:\n\n    curl -LO https://releases.w3ft.net/weftd/v0.31.0/weftd_0.31.0_amd64.deb\n    sudo dpkg -i weftd_0.31.0_amd64.deb\n\n  arm64 packages are also available -- just swap the suffix in the paths above.\n\n  The package installs the systemd unit and enables it on boot, but does not\n  start it: weftd won't run until it's configured.\n\n  \x1b[38;5;208m2. Create a token\x1b[0m\n  Create a namespaced token as described in Connect a log\n  source (man namespaces). Put token: in\n  /etc/weftd/config.yaml, or supply it as WEFTD_TOKEN (for example in\n  /etc/default/weftd). The environment value wins, so a secret injector can\n  provide the token without templating the YAML.\n\n  \x1b[38;5;208m3. Configure and start\x1b[0m\n  weftd reads /etc/weftd/config.yaml. A minimal config needs only a token and\n  at least one source:\n\n    # /etc/weftd/config.yaml\n    token: wft_a1b2c3d4e5f6...\n\n    sources:\n      - service: myapp\n        path: /var/log/myapp/app.log\n\n  Then start it with sudo systemctl start weftd. Rows arrive within a few\n  seconds. weftd requires a schema, so approve\n  one (man overview) once the source has\n  sampled.\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mFinding your logs\x1b[0m\n  Weft maps weftd sources to log files as follows:\n\n  \x1b[38;5;8mweftd source                            weft file\x1b[0m\n  \x1b[38;5;8m--------------------------------------  --------------------------------------\x1b[0m\n  path: /var/log/myapp/api.log            /log/<ns>/api/main.log\n  path: /var/log/myapp/api.log, service:  /log/<ns>/myapp/main.log\n  myapp\n  path: /var/log/myapp/audit.log,         /log/<ns>/myapp/audit.log\n  service: myapp, kind: audit\n  unit: myapp-worker.service              /log/<ns>/myapp-worker/main.log\n  path: /var/log/myapp/*.log              /log/<ns>/api/main.log,\n                                          /log/<ns>/worker/main.log, one per\n                                          file\n  path: /var/log/*/access.log             /log/<ns>/api/access/main.log,\n                                          /log/<ns>/web/access/main.log\n  deployment: web with a metrics: block   /log/<ns>/web/host.log, disk.log and\n                                          network.log\n\n  Every row carries instance, the host's name, so a single\n  tail /log/<ns>/myapp/main.log covers every host.\n\n  service: is ignored on a glob; the service is derived per file from its path\n  below the pattern's fixed directory, minus the extension. To make several\n  files share one service, list them as separate non-glob sources with the same\n  service:.\n\n  (The presence of the <env> segment depends on how you created your token.\n  See Paths (man introduction) for the layout rules.)\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mConfiguration\x1b[0m\n  General, box-level settings are top-level keys:\n\n  \x1b[38;5;8mKey            Definition                      Default\x1b[0m\n  \x1b[38;5;8m-------------  ------------------------------  -------------------------------\x1b[0m\n  token          Ingest token for your           none; required\n                 namespace. May instead come\n                 from the WEFTD_TOKEN\n                 environment variable (see\n                 Connect a host).\n  auto_update    Self-update from                true\n                 releases.w3ft.net (see\n                 Updates). false pins this host\n                 to the installed version.\n  deployment     The fleet or scaling unit this  empty\n                 agent belongs to (k8s\n                 Deployment, AWS ASG, etc.).\n                 Stamped on every row, and the\n                 service host metrics are\n                 written under.\n  region         Region stamped on every row.    auto-detected from cloud\n                                                 metadata at startup\n  socket         Path of the records socket for  none; socket disabled\n                 your apps (see App API).\n  metrics        Host metrics: cpu, memory,      no block: off\n                 disk, load, network. Having\n                 the block turns them on (see\n                 System metrics).\n  metavariables  Values for the deployment,      none\n                 version, region, cluster and\n                 node columns, each read from\n                 the environment, a file or a\n                 JSON file (see Metavariables).\n                 Example: version: {file:\n                 /opt/myapp/VERSION}\n  checkpoint     Tuning for                      see that section\n                 resume-after-restart, which is\n                 always on (see Resuming after\n                 a restart).\n  sources        The list of things to tail.     none; required\n\n  \x1b[38;5;208mSources\x1b[0m\n  A host can have any number of sources, listed under the sources key. Each\n  entry tails one thing. Set exactly one of path or unit.\n\n    sources:\n      - service: myapp\n        path: /var/log/myapp/app.log\n        version:\n          file: /opt/myapp/VERSION\n\n      - service: nginx\n        path: /var/log/nginx/access.log\n\n      - unit: myapp-worker.service\n        kind: main\n\n  \x1b[38;5;8mKey            Definition                      Default\x1b[0m\n  \x1b[38;5;8m-------------  ------------------------------  -------------------------------\x1b[0m\n  path           A file or glob to tail.         none\n                 Mutually exclusive with unit.\n  unit           A systemd unit to read from     none\n                 the journal. Mutually\n                 exclusive with path.\n  service        The <service> segment of the    file name without extension, or\n                 source's file path in weft      unit name without .service\n                 (see Finding your logs).\n                 Ignored on a glob.\n  kind           The file part of the            main\n                 pseudofile,\n                 /log/<ns>/<service>/<kind>.log.\n                 heartbeats, runtime, host,\n                 disk and network are reserved\n                 (the last three hold host\n                 metrics).\n  metavariables  This source's own values for    none\n                 deployment, version, region,\n                 cluster and node, overriding\n                 the host-wide ones (see\n                 Metavariables).\n\n  Every source, whatever its kind, handles lines the same way. CRLF endings are\n  removed, blank lines are skipped, and leading and trailing whitespace is\n  trimmed from every line, so indentation (a stack trace's leading spaces, say)\n  does not survive. A line longer than 1 MiB is cut at 1 MiB and ends with\n  …[line truncated].\n\n  Some behaviors vary by source type:\n\n  - For concrete files (path: /var/log/myapp/app.log), the tailer keeps exact byte\n    offsets, follows logrotate in both styles (rename-and-recreate and\n    copytruncate), and drains the old file before it switches to the new one.\n    A line is shipped only once its newline has been written.\n  - For glob patterns (path: /var/log/myapp/*.log), *, ? and [...] work as in\n    the shell, and ** matches any number of directories. Files that exist at\n    startup are tailed from the saved position. A file created later in the\n    directory the pattern starts from is picked up automatically and read from\n    its first line. A file created in a deeper directory after startup is\n    found at the next restart. If a concrete file is also named by its own\n    non-glob source, that explicit source wins and the glob skips it. Each\n    matched file behaves as a single file does.\n  - For systemd unit (unit: myapp.service), weftd runs\n    journalctl -f -u <unit> and ships new journal lines from the moment it\n    starts. Unit sources are not checkpointed (see\n    Delivery below), so a restart does not replay the journal, and\n    journalctl must be on the host.\n\n  \x1b[38;5;208mMetavariables\x1b[0m\n  Weft refers to dimensions as metavariables (man metavars) because of the\n  first-class support they have in our awk and jq implementations. Using weftd\n  enables you to set more of them than via other producers. The configuration for\n  this lives in weftd.yaml.\n\n  region comes from a top-level config key when set. When not set, weftd asks\n  the cloud metadata service once at startup when running on AWS, GCP, or Azure. Another set of metavariables (deployment, version, region, cluster and\n  node) can be set for the whole host, or for a single source, via one of three\n  mechanisms:\n\n  \x1b[38;5;8mKey   Behavior                             Refreshed\x1b[0m\n  \x1b[38;5;8m----  -----------------------------------  -----------------------------------\x1b[0m\n  env   weftd reads the supplied             Once, at startup. Changing it needs\n        environment variable                 a weftd restart.\n  file  weftd reads the contents of the      Whenever the file changes.\n        file and trims whitespace\n  json  weftd reads JSON from a file and     Whenever the file changes.\n        extract a value at the supplied\n        json path\n\n  Examples:\n\n    metavariables:\n      version:\n        file: /opt/myapp/VERSION          # your deploy writes e.g. \"2026.10.18-3\"\n      node:\n        json: /opt/app/data/shard.json:.shard_id\n      cluster:\n        env: CLUSTER_NAME\n\n  A json: path is a minimal subset of jq: field access (.foo), nested\n  fields (.foo.bar) and array indices (.items[0].name). For anything\n  richer, pre-process the file with your own tooling and point weftd at the\n  result.\n\n  If you have metavariables defined at both the host level and for individual\n  sources, the source values will win. Rows that belong to no source, such as\n  host metrics, only ever get the host-wide values.\n\n    metavariables:\n      cluster:\n        env: CLUSTER_NAME               # every source on this host\n\n    sources:\n      - service: api\n        path: /var/log/api/*.log\n        metavariables:\n          version:\n            file: /opt/api/VERSION      # just api\n      - service: worker\n        path: /var/log/worker.log\n        metavariables:\n          version:\n            file: /opt/worker/VERSION   # just worker\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mOther features\x1b[0m\n  \x1b[38;5;208mSystem metrics (optional)\x1b[0m\n  weftd can also ship host metrics alongside log lines. Add a top-level\n  metrics: block to turn them on, and delete it to turn them off. An empty\n  block (metrics: {}) runs everything with the defaults:\n\n    # /etc/weftd/config.yaml\n    token: wft_a1b2c3d4e5f6...\n    deployment: web\n\n    metrics:\n      interval: 30s\n\n    sources:\n      - service: api\n        path: /var/log/api/*.log\n\n  \x1b[38;5;8mKey         Definition                                                Default\x1b[0m\n  \x1b[38;5;8m----------  --------------------------------------------------------  --------\x1b[0m\n  interval    How often to sample, as a duration. A value that doesn't  30s\n              parse falls back to the default.\n  collectors  Which of cpu, memory, disk, load, network to run.         all five\n              Unknown names are ignored.\n\n  Samples are read from /proc (Linux only) and streamed as JSON log rows.\n  Metrics go under your deployment, as if it were a service.\n  A host with deployment: web writes\n  /log/<namespace>/web/{host,disk,network}.log. deployment has to be set for\n  metrics to run; without one weftd logs an error and writes nothing. Depending\n  on which collectors are enabled, your metrics will end up in:\n\n  \x1b[38;5;8mFile         One row per                      Contents\x1b[0m\n  \x1b[38;5;8m-----------  -------------------------------  --------------------------------\x1b[0m\n  host.log     tick                             cpu, memory and load objects\n  disk.log     /dev/* mount, per tick           path, total_gb, used_gb, pct\n  network.log  non-loopback interface, per      iface, rx_bytes_sec,\n               tick                             tx_bytes_sec\n\n  The objects in host.log:\n\n  \x1b[38;5;8mObject  Fields\x1b[0m\n  \x1b[38;5;8m------  --------------------------------------------------\x1b[0m\n  cpu     user, system, idle, iowait (percent), cores\n  memory  total_mb, used_mb, available_mb, swap_used_mb, pct\n  load    load1, load5, load15\n\n  host.log holds cpu, memory and load as nested objects, so the first\n  tick has no cpu (it needs two samples to compute a delta) and a collector you\n  turned off has no object. network.log needs two samples too, so it's empty\n  for the first tick after startup.\n\n    # Recent memory pressure\n    tail /log/myns/web/host.log | jq '.memory.pct'\n\n    # Per-host, per-mount disk usage, alerting above 80%\n    tail /log/myns/web/disk.log \\\n      | jq -r '\"\\($host):\\(.path) \\(.pct)\"' \\\n      | alert above 80 -k 2 -t 1 --notify=ops\n\n    # Recent load averages by host\n    tail -n 1000 /log/myns/web/host.log | jq '{host: $host, load5: .load.load5}'\n\n    # One host's disks\n    tail /log/myns/web/disk.log | jq 'select($host == \"i-0abc\")'\n\n  Metrics only run on Linux (the collectors read /proc/stat,\n  /proc/meminfo, /proc/mounts, /proc/loadavg, and\n  /proc/net/dev). On other kernels the block is ignored.\n\n  \x1b[38;5;208mApp API\x1b[0m\n  weftd can listen on a local Unix socket so your own applications can send\n  events, heartbeats and runtime stats without a token or a network call. Name the\n  socket in config.yaml:\n\n    # /etc/weftd/config.yaml\n    socket: /var/run/weftd.sock\n\n  The Go and Python SDKs find /var/run/weftd.sock on their own; if you name it\n  something else, set WEFT_SOCKET to match. See the SDK READMEs for usage:\n  Go (https://github.com/w3ft-net/weft-go) and Python (pip install w3ft).\n\n  \x1b[38;5;208mSDK log files\x1b[0m\n  The Go and Python SDKs can write your application's logs to a file with\n  each line's timestamp, level and trace ID in a header, and weftd reads those\n  values straight off it. Point the SDK at a file with WEFT_LOG_FILE (or the\n  path argument to InstallLogging / install_logging) and name the same file as\n  a source:\n\n    sources:\n      - service: myapp\n        path: /var/log/myapp/weft.log\n\n  A service fed this way has no schema to extract: weft configures it as soon as\n  the first line arrives, and weft source schema says which values came from\n  the SDK. The SDK can also capture stdout and stderr into the file; they\n  appear as the service's stdout and stderr logs. The file belongs to the\n  SDK, so don't write to it from anything else, and if you capture stdout and\n  stderr, stop a systemd unit from redirecting them to a file too.\n\n  A service is either SDK-written or not: don't point an SDK file and a plain\n  log file at the same service.\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mBehavior and semantics\x1b[0m\n  \x1b[38;5;208mDelivery\x1b[0m\n  Files are the only source with delivery guarantees. weftd keeps recently sent\n  rows (up to 8 MiB per file) until the coordinator acknowledges them, at the\n  commit_interval below, and only then advances the saved position. Anything\n  unacknowledged is resent on a new stream, so delivery is at-least-once:\n  duplicates are possible, gaps are not. During an outage the log file is the\n  buffer. weftd stops reading when its window is full and resumes when the\n  coordinator is back, and there is no disk spool of its own. Unit\n  (journalctl), records-socket and metrics rows are best-effort: they are\n  sent straight through and are not replayed after a restart or an outage.\n  The socket drops lines when its 4096-line queue is full.\n\n  \x1b[38;5;208mResuming after a restart\x1b[0m\n  weftd remembers how far it has delivered each tailed file, so a restart picks\n  up where it left off and reads whatever was written in the meantime. The saved\n  position means \"weft acknowledged everything up to here\", so lines\n  that were in flight when weftd stopped are sent again.\n\n  State is kept in /var/lib/weftd/checkpoints/. The .deb unit creates it\n  (StateDirectory=weftd). Checkpointing is always on. These settings, all\n  optional, tune it:\n\n    checkpoint:\n      dir: /var/lib/weftd/checkpoints   # where state.json lives\n      flush_interval: 5s                # how often positions are written to disk\n      commit_interval: 15s              # how often delivered lines are acknowledged\n      max_resume_bytes: 256MiB          # per-file cap on backlog replayed after downtime\n\n  What weftd does for each file when it starts:\n\n  \x1b[38;5;8mSituation                               Starts at\x1b[0m\n  \x1b[38;5;8m--------------------------------------  --------------------------------------\x1b[0m\n  First start with checkpointing (no      the end of the file\n  state yet)\n  File has no saved position (appeared    the beginning\n  while weftd was down)\n  File matches its saved position         the saved position\n  File is shorter than the saved          the beginning\n  position (truncated)\n  File was replaced while weftd was down  the beginning; the old file's unread\n  (rotated)                               tail is not recovered\n  More than max_resume_bytes behind       the newest max_resume_bytes, from a\n                                          line boundary\n\n  A file is matched by device, inode and a hash of its first kilobyte, so a\n  reused inode is not mistaken for the same file. Files created while weftd is\n  running are read from their first line. Every skip or replay is logged, and\n  summarized once a minute as a checkpoint stats line per file.\n\n  Notes:\n\n  - Containers: the state directory must survive the pod. On Kubernetes,\n    mount /var/lib/weftd from a hostPath. An emptyDir or the container\n    filesystem is wiped on every reschedule.\n  - Two weftd on one host cannot share a state directory. The second one\n    logs that the directory is locked and runs without checkpointing.\n  - A damaged state.json is moved to state.json.corrupt and weftd starts\n    as if freshly installed.\n\n  \x1b[38;5;208mUpdates\x1b[0m\n  Once a day weftd checks releases.w3ft.net for a newer release. By default it\n  downloads the build for the host's platform, verifies its SHA-256, replaces the\n  running binary, and exits so systemd (Restart=always) relaunches it. Set\n  auto_update: false to pin a host, for example while you investigate a\n  release. It then only logs a line when a newer version exists.\n"

